<https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-facebook-2012-2013> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Dated statements by the studio principal on Facebook in 2012 and 2013, with the times Facebook assigned them, and a public fundraising page he placed on the USA Projects site in 2013, captured by the Internet Archive. In public comments on posts by a New York art critic he wrote 'Coming Soon: Art Market Reform ! ! !' on 2012-08-14 at 05:20:21 UTC; 'Only thing better than that, would be open source, free, participatory, interactive, multi-media 21st century MetaConceptual Art, which wouldn't cost a dime, and could be open and available for the planet Earth 24/7/365, just like McDonalds' on 2012-11-16 at 07:25:18 UTC; '\" Art Market Reform \" sounds like profanity to many here among us' on 2012-11-21 at 12:50:29 UTC; and '#WTF is #Art ?! give thanks !' on 2012-11-21 at 14:34:42 UTC. In private messages he wrote on 2012-11-25 that he had started a Facebook page one or two days earlier, and that 'Art Historians from the Future is a simple example' of starting with analog art and changing it digitally; on 2013-05-29 that 'One of my more recent works of Metaconceptual Art is titled \"Art Market Reform\"'; and on 2013-07-23 that 'I am the creator of Metaconceptual Art and this is a Meta Art Work', of a work called 'The Art Market Bubble', closing with 'For the Love of God ... We are the Art.' The USA Projects page, 'Pittsburgh: City of Bridges, City of God', credited to Sun and Rain Works and captured on 2013-08-12, asks for support 'in the tradition of Metaconceptual Art' and gives its author's biography as 'I am a contemporary American metaconceptual Artist.' Together they carry several of the collection's titles and phrases earlier than any date this collection had held for them." .
