<https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-inscription-photograph-2011> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P138i_has_representation> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-inscription-photograph-2011#visual-item> .
<https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-inscription-photograph-2011> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "One digital photograph, held by the studio, taken with a camera phone and recording its capture as 2011-11-08 12:18:15. It shows two works laid one on the other. On top is a narrow board inscribed by hand in purple marker: '\"Helping the Art Historians of the future to keep their Employment\" (Here and Now. ~)'. Beneath it is a large sheet covered in small handwritten notes running at many angles, with a coloured spiral and strokes of coloured pencil; along its right-hand edge, written in quotation marks, it reads '\"W.T.F. is Art ?\"'. The studio identifies the board as the work 'Helping the Art Historians of the future' and the sheet as 'Dancing with Mummies', a title it gives as a reference to Thomas Hoving, the former director of the Metropolitan Museum of Art, and to what it calls its archaeology of the art world. Neither physical work survives, by the studio's account, and this photograph is the only known record of the sheet. The board was the foundation of a digital work, ArtHistoriansOTheFuture, which the studio posted publicly to Flickr on 2013-06-02. The title is named earlier, in a private Facebook message of 2012-11-25 giving 'Art Historians from the Future' as an example of starting with analog art and changing it digitally; and a public fundraising page on the USA Projects site, captured by the Internet Archive on 2013-08-12, closes its appeal with the board's own words, 'Together we are helping the Art historians of the future to keep their employment, here and now'. Both are set out in the record of the 2012-2013 Facebook and USA Projects evidence. The photograph dates the phrase W.T.F. is Art, written on a physical work, to November 2011 at the latest, and the board's phrase to nineteen months before that posting and twenty-two months before the studio used the same words in its letter of 2013-09-12 to a university art museum." .
