<https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-postcard-work-2008> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "One digital photograph, held by the studio, of a small work made on a commercial museum postcard and shown framed under glass. The picture side has been painted over in acrylic and ink with a single large flower in yellow and orange over a purple ground, with green leaves below, and is signed in ink 'Joe Chirum' and dated '08'. The address side carries a handwritten inscription in ink: '\"For the Love of Art, these tulips are beautiful! Almost as good as in real life\"', followed by the initials 'J.M.C.' and '08', and a postscript, 'P.S. \"Double the Fun\"'. The printed matter of the postcard remains visible beneath the writing and identifies it as one of a series of Impressionist painting postcards published by an art museum in Chicago, reproducing a painting of tulips by Cezanne. The photograph was taken on 2011-11-16 by the same camera phone that took the photograph of the sheet inscribed 'W.T.F. is Art ?' eight days earlier. The studio dates the work itself to 2008, which is the date written on it; the photograph shows only that the work existed by November 2011." .
