<https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-sothebys-approach-2008> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A thread of eleven messages between the studio principal and a junior cataloguer in the Photographs department at Sotheby's, New York, running from 2008-08-14 to 2009-03-19, together with the 3 MB attachment sent with the first of them. The studio's opening message introduces its author as an artist living in Eugene, Oregon and sole proprietor of an art business named Sun and Rain Works, states that he owns an Ansel Adams print and is researching its historical and artistic significance, describes the attached photographs as made with a 1.3 megapixel FujiFilm camera, and is signed off 'For the Love of Art'. Sotheby's replies the next day offering to include the print in its April 2009 sale at a provisional estimate of $7,000 to $10,000, gives a consignment deadline of end of January 2009, names its department administrator, and attaches its consignment charges. A month later the cataloguer writes again to ask whether the owner wishes to proceed, naming the work as 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks'. The studio answers the following day that it is interested in consigning, that it is in Hawai'i at the time, and that it will arrange shipment itself. The attachment is held and has been examined; the messages are held as screenshots of a mail client. Two further messages, of 2009-03-11 and 2009-03-12, follow the consignment through to the sale: the department's administrator writes that the catalogue has gone out and that the signed consignment agreement posted in early February has not come back, and the studio replies that the consignment sheet was received and will be faxed, giving a general-delivery address in Eugene and explaining a delay in mail forwarded to Maui." .
<https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-sothebys-approach-2008> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P1_is_identified_by> _:r30b0 .
