_:r0b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r0b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r0b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "work:art-is-capital" .
_:r0b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r0b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r0b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A painting by Sun & Rain Works carrying the words 'Art is Capital' in painted letters. It is known to this collection from a photograph the studio published on its own website, which the Internet Archive captured on 2014-12-18 and again on 2016-01-11, and from the studio's listing of it on the Saatchi Art site, which the Internet Archive captured on 2014-05-22. No image of the painting is published with this record. The listing gives it as an acrylic painting on canvas of 2014, 12 by 9 by 0.5 inches; those are the studio's own entries on a third party's site, not a measurement taken for this record." .
_:r0b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r0b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r0b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "How it is dated, and how far that reaches. The photograph sits on the studio's site at sites/all/themes/corporateclean/mockup/slide-1.jpg and is held by the Internet Archive at https://web.archive.org/web/20141218140553id_/http://wtfisart.com/sites/all/themes/corporateclean/mockup/slide-1.jpg, 81,058 bytes, 500 by 250 pixels. Above it the site's slider reads 'Art is Capital invest with confidence'. What that establishes is that the painting existed and had been photographed by 2014-12-18, on the evidence of a third party with no stake in this collection, and that it was still on the site on 2016-01-11. What it does not establish is when the painting was made, which the capture cannot reach, or anything about the object itself beyond what a banner-shaped crop shows." .
_:r0b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r0b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r0b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The Saatchi Art listing, captured 2014-05-22. The studio listed the painting for sale on Saatchi Art, the online gallery of the Saatchi Gallery, under its artist account 578159, and the Internet Archive captured the listing at 2014-05-22 07:18:32 UTC, at https://web.archive.org/web/20140522071832/http://www.saatchiart.com:80/art/Painting-Art-is-Capital/578159/2025103/view. The page is titled 'Saatchi Art Artist: Joseph Chirum; Acrylic 2014 Painting \"Art is Capital\"'; its description reads 'Metaconceptual Art Acrylic on Canvas', its tags include 'Metaconceptual Art' and 'Metaconceptual', and it gives the work as 12 by 9 by 0.5 inches on canvas, the original offered at $1,000 and prints from $180. The capture as retrieved on 2026-09-24, 79,508 bytes, has SHA-256 c41ba52d59869fe90c5c77458809a320901bc484ca3620a72251a400d26a5efc. What it adds is a date assigned by parties other than the studio: the painting existed, under this title and described as Metaconceptual Art, by 22 May 2014, seven months before the website capture that had been the only source for it. It agrees with the studio's Flickr account, which carries a photograph titled 'ArtisCapital9x12Acrylic' uploaded on 2014-05-18. What it does not add: the medium, size and the year '2014' are the studio's own entries on the listing, not an examination of the work, and the year is the year the studio gave the painting, not a date of making that anyone else fixed. The listing records an offer for sale, not a sale. The same page links nine more of the account's works - Art Market Reform in acrylic and in watercolor, Art Tax in paint and in charcoal, What Hedge Funds ?! twice, Allegory of the Cave Art, Flesh and Bones and Mon Incline - but none of those pages, and not the artist's own page, was captured, so they are known only as titles in this one page's links." .
_:r0b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r0b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r0b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What is not held. This collection holds no photograph of the painting other than the archived crop, which is 500 by 250 pixels and therefore a detail rather than documentation of the whole work. It holds no measurement or statement of medium other than the studio's own entries on the Saatchi Art listing, no record of where the painting is, and nothing dated before 2014 that carries the title. The studio has stated, in 2026, that its works exist and are held in its own collection; that statement covers this one no more and no less than the others, and nobody outside the studio has examined it. If the painting is photographed and measured, this record should be replaced by a fuller one." .
_:r0b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r0b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r10b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: the approach to the Ansel Adams organisation (8-9 August 2008)" .
_:r10b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r10b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r10b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r10b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-adams-gallery-2008" .
_:r10b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r10b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r10b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The sign-off, moved back. The valediction for the love of Art closes both messages of 2008-08-08, and so predates by two days the earliest previously documented use of it. The auction-house record treats the phrase at length, as the studio's own adaptation of the title of Damien Hirst's For the Love of God, and observes that it closed both approaches of August 2008 and was therefore a settled signature rather than a one-off. That observation is strengthened: the phrase now closes four messages, to three organisations, across six days, and is present in the earliest documents the collection holds. What it is not, here as there, is a reference anyone was invited to see. The studio's account of the Hirst adaptation is its own, given in 2026, and is reported as that account rather than as a fact these messages establish." .
_:r10b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r10b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r10b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What these documents establish, and what they do not. They establish that by the evening of 2008-08-08 the studio principal held the Ansel Adams print, had photographed it earlier that day, and was sending those photographs to the organisation bearing the photographer's name; that the archive later sent to the auction house existed in its final form by that evening; that the contact followed an earlier approach made by telephone; and that the sign-off used throughout the August 2008 outreach was in use from the earliest documented moment. They do not touch Metaconceptual Art, which is named in neither. They do not describe the print, the photographs or the approach as an artwork, a performance or a project of any kind: one message offers assistance, the other asks after the recipient and sends files. And they carry no designation of the auction, which had been proposed to nobody when they were written." .
_:r10b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r10b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r10b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Redaction and scope. Following this collection's convention, the representative at the Adams organisation, who was addressed in an official capacity, is identified by role only; their given name and mailbox are withheld pending consent and retained in the studio's private copy. Both correspondents' mailbox addresses are replaced, and the studio principal's telephone numbers are withheld as personal data, though the fact that two numbers are given, that they differ in one digit, and that one is given 'again', is preserved because it bears on the order of the messages. The final octet of the sending provider's X-Originating-IP stamp is withheld; the header itself and the rest of its value are retained, because the presence of that stamp is what distinguishes the two files' provenance and is the point of the record. The domain anseladams.com is deliberately retained: the identity of the organisation approached is what gives the record its weight. No endorsement by that organisation of this project, of Metaconceptual Art, or of any claim in this collection is implied or should be inferred. It was asked in September 2026 whether it retained either message. On 2026-09-07 its customer support team, identified here by role only, replied that the organisation changed email servers in 2015 and lost all email from before 2015, and so could not help. That is a negative with a stated scope: the organisation holds no email of 2008 at all, so its inability to confirm these messages says nothing either way about whether they were received. It said nothing else about them. The unredacted originals are not published; their SHA-256 values are, so the held files can later be shown to be unaltered without their contents being disclosed." .
_:r10b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r10b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r10b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r10b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r10b14 .
_:r10b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r10b13 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the Adams organisation approach record" .
_:r10b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r10b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r10b14 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r10b14 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r10b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Two outbound messages to the same named representative at anseladams.com, sent within about twenty minutes of each other on the evening of 2008-08-08 Pacific time, from the studio principal's then-current hotmail.com mailbox. The first as the headers order them, under the subject 'Ansel Adams San Francisco', carries eight loose JPEG photographs of the Ansel Adams print. The second, under the subject 'Ansel Adams Visits SanFrancisco', carries a single attachment: the archive San Francisco.zip, byte-identical to the copy the studio holds and to the one sent to the auction house six days later. Both describe the camera as a 1.3 megapixel FujiFilm, both invite a telephone call in preference to email, both give a telephone number, and both close for the love of Art. Together they are the earliest documents held in this collection, preceding the approach to the museum photography department by two days and the approach to the auction house by five." .
_:r10b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r10b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r10b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The two messages are not equally well evidenced, and the difference is the most important thing in this record. The message carrying the archive retains its original Hotmail headers: an X-Originating-IP stamped by the sending provider, a Date bearing a real local offset of minus seven hours, a GUID-style MIME boundary of the kind Windows Live Hotmail generated, and no Message-ID, Thread-Index, Content-Language or Exchange organisation headers at all. The message carrying the loose photographs has the opposite profile: its Date is normalised to UTC, and it carries a Message-ID whose host is Exchange Online, together with Thread-Index and X-MS-Exchange-Organization headers. Exchange Online did not exist in 2008. So one of these two files has passed through a later platform that rewrote its headers, and the other has not. The archive message is, on its headers, a 2008 artefact. The photographs message is a 2008 message as preserved by a present-day mailbox." .
_:r10b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r10b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r10b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A period marker in the archive message, stated for what it is worth. Its footer advertises the Windows Live Hotmail Mobile Summer Games Trivia Contest, linking to gowindowslive.com. The Beijing Olympic Games opened on 2008-08-08, the date the message bears, and ran to 2008-08-24. A promotional footer tied to that event is consistent with the stated date and would be an odd thing to reconstruct afterwards. It is corroboration of period rather than of the particular day, and it is not independent of the sender, since the footer travelled with the message he sent." .
_:r10b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r10b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r10b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Their order is not settled, and this record does not settle it. The headers place the photographs message about twenty minutes before the archive message. The texts point the other way. The archive message reads as a first delivery: it opens with a greeting and an enquiry after the recipient, describes the photographs as the best the writer could manage, and states a telephone number plainly. The photographs message reads as a second attempt: it says the number is being given 'again', and sends eight of the eleven frames loose rather than archived, which is what a sender does when an archive has not worked. Against that, the two messages give telephone numbers differing in a single digit, so one of them contains a typing error and neither can be used to order them. The honest position is that two messages were sent to the same person within twenty-odd minutes on one evening, that the timestamp of one of them has been rewritten by a later system, and that which came first is not established. Nothing in this collection depends on the answer." .
_:r10b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r10b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r10b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The archive ties this approach to the auction-house record, and does so by content rather than by assertion. The attachment to the 2008-08-08 message is byte-identical to San Francisco.zip, whose SHA-256 the auction-house record already published as the 3 MB attachment sent to the Photographs department on 2008-08-14. The same archive therefore went to the Ansel Adams organisation six days before it went to the trade. Its eleven frames carry EXIF DateTimeOriginal values running from 2008-08-08 00:05:33 to 00:10:22, a single session of about five minutes; the EXIF camera model is FUJIFILM FinePix1300, which agrees with both messages' description of a 1.3 megapixel camera. The eight loose photographs in the other message are byte-identical to eight members of that same archive. Three frames, DSCF0014, DSCF0015 and DSCF0023, were not sent loose. DSCF0020, the frame composing the print with Warhol's philosophy, was sent both ways." .
_:r10b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r10b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r10b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "It answers an open question in the museum record, and the two records corroborate each other. The museum letter of 2008-08-11 reports that its writer is in contact with a named person at what he calls 'the Ansel Adams Musem in SanFrancisco'. The museum record notes that no institution of that name was operating in San Francisco in 2008, the Ansel Adams Center for Photography having closed in 2001, and left open which body was meant. These messages identify it, and the museum letter, now held as a file, names the person in full, so the identification no longer rests on inference at all. They are addressed to a named representative at anseladams.com and they predate the museum letter, which is the order that letter's own past tense requires. The misnaming is resolved as a misnaming: the organisation approached bore the photographer's name and was not a San Francisco museum. The weight lies in the independence of the artefacts. The museum letter is held as a screenshot of a mail client; these are held as mailbox exports. Neither was produced from the other, neither needed to mention the other, and they agree on a checkable fact." .
_:r10b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r10b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r10b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Contact began before either message. The photographs message gives a telephone number 'again', and the archive message opens 'How are you doing', asks nothing and explains nothing about who is writing or why, and offers the photographs as though they had been asked for. Both presume a relationship already established. So the engagement with the Adams organisation began before 2008-08-08, and on the evidence of both messages it began by telephone. Nothing of that earlier contact is held: no note, no call record, no reply from the organisation to anything. The earliest documents in this collection are therefore not the beginning of the engagement they document, and no claim about a first approach should rest on them." .
_:r10b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r10b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r10b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The subject lines, and what is not yet in them. These two messages are headed 'Ansel Adams San Francisco' and 'Ansel Adams Visits SanFrancisco'. The messages of 2008-08-11 and 2008-08-14, to the museum and to the auction house, are headed 'Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco', and the auction house's replies carry that subject forward. So across six days the heading moves from a plain statement of the subject, through a personification of the photographer as a visitor, to a claim that he finds Art there. The progression is recorded and not interpreted. It is consistent with a framing that developed over that week, and equally consistent with a writer trying different headings on different correspondents; nothing held distinguishes the two. The art-inflected heading is not present at the earliest date." .
_:r10b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r10b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: the studio's websites as captured by the Internet Archive (2013-2016)" .
_:r11b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r11b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r11b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r11b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-archived-websites-2013-2016" .
_:r11b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r11b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r11b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the sunandrainworks.com captures show. The root capture of 2013-05-22 is a bare Drupal installation with no content. By 2016-01-26 the front page carries two posts, and the page 'Beginnings of an Art Odyssey', captured separately on 2016-03-27, sets out all eight defining characteristics of Metaconceptual Art in full: the returning of the formless to form; the commodification of art and its relation to the global economic crisis; the power structures of the art world and its institutions; an archaeology of the art world and art history; the interplay of time and space; transcendence of medium to arrive within the collective consciousness; the annihilating of the boundaries between art-world insiders and outsiders; and the annihilating of the boundaries between art and life. This is the same set, in the same order and substantially the same words, as the posting of 2012 recorded in the postings evidence record, which is itself independently preserved. The site's own stamps give 'Submitted by Rainbow on Sun, 10/04/2015' for that page and 'Fri, 11/14/2014' for its sibling; those are the site's account of itself, and the capture dates are the part no one in the studio controls." .
_:r11b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The Flickr account, and the photograph of the notice board. The studio's public Flickr account, under the display name Joe Chirum and the user name LivingArt8, was joined in March 2008 and holds some seven hundred photographs, most of them the studio's tie-dye and batik work and its paintings. It is a working photostream rather than a record of this project, and nothing in it should be read as evidence about the auction. One photograph in it does bear on this collection: the notice board published here as Art Notes, titled 'Art Notes' and marked 'Uploaded on June 2, 2013', at https://www.flickr.com/photos/24377024@N07/8921139312/ . The image is pixel-identical to the studio's held original. So the board, with the line 'WTF is Art ?!' on it, was publicly posted on a third party's platform three months before the museum letter of 2013-09-12, at an address anyone can check, on a date the platform assigned and the studio did not. It was not, on any evidence here, publicly read: that page records eight views in the thirteen years since. Flickr's 'Taken on June 1, 2013' on that photograph is not a capture date: it is the value Flickr supplies when an uploaded file carries no Exif, and the copy uploaded had been stripped. The studio's own original gives 2012-11-30, and the Art Notes record sets out how that date is bounded." .
_:r11b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What else the photostream carries, enumerated on 2026-09-12, with its dates and its limits. Nine photographs in the account bear on this collection, and their upload dates fall in two groups. On 2013-06-02, in the same upload session as the Art Notes board, the account posted a digital work titled 'ArtHistoriansOTheFuture', at https://www.flickr.com/photos/24377024@N07/8920785186/ , which is the inscription of a studio board recoloured over a rainbow ground and whose words recur in the museum letter of 2013-09-12; the inscription-photograph record describes the board it was made from, photographed in 2011. The same session posted a painting titled 'WhatHedgeFunds(AcryliconCardboard)', at https://www.flickr.com/photos/24377024@N07/8921172858/ . That is a work carrying the title What Hedge Funds, publicly posted three months before the museum letter. The registrar's receipts record independently dates the registration of WHATHEDGEFUNDS.COM to 2011-10-16, and the 2014 capture described above shows the studio's own site publishing the line 'What Hedge Funds ?! invest in Art'. Three unrelated third parties - a photo host, a domain registrar and the Internet Archive - therefore fix three separate dates against that one title, and the studio set none of them. On 2014-05-18 the account posted six further works: 'ArtMarketReform7x10Watercolor' and 'ArtMarketReform9x12Acrylic', 'ArtTax7x10Watercolor1' and 'ArtTax7x10Watercolor2', and 'WhatHedgeFunds12x16Acrylic' and 'WhatHedgeFunds6x8Acrylic', with media and dimensions given in the titles and, on three of them, in a description field reading 'Acrylic on Canvas' or 'Mixed Media'. Art Market Reform and Art Tax are the two works the museum letter of 2013-09-12 names as Metaconceptual Art. These photographs postdate that letter by eight months, so they are consistent with it and are not evidence for it: what they establish is that the works the letter named exist as physical objects, not that they existed when it was written. Three of the 2014 photographs carry the public tag 'Metaconceptual'. It is not the earliest public use of the term with a date assigned by a third party: the Pastebin postings of 2011 and 2012 are earlier, and so are a public Facebook comment of 2012-11-16 and a fundraising page on the USA Projects site, captured by the Internet Archive on 2013-08-12, whose author's biography reads 'I am a contemporary American metaconceptual Artist', both set out in the record of the 2012-2013 Facebook and USA Projects evidence. The 2013 letter is earlier too but was private correspondence, and the profile biography that uses the term is undated and unarchived. What the 2014 tags add is the term attached, publicly, to photographs of the works themselves. One caution applies to all eight files: each shows a 'Taken on' value exactly one day before its upload date, which is Flickr's fallback for a file carrying no Exif. None of them carries a camera date, and the upload stamp is the only date any of them has." .
_:r11b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The Flickr biography, and why it carries no date. The same account's profile biography states that in May 2009 the artist auctioned an artwork at a Sotheby's New York photograph auction, lot 16, 'as a performance act of metaconceptual Art', titled 'WTF is Ansel Adams ?!'. That is the designation this collection records, in the artist's own words, on a public page. It cannot be dated. A profile biography can be rewritten at any time and keeps no visible history, and the Internet Archive holds no capture of this profile at all, which was checked on 2026-09-11. It is therefore a public assertion of unknown date, and it repeats the same error as the 2013 letter in giving the sale as May 2009 when the catalogue gives 30 March 2009. Both are left as they stand, for the reason the museum correspondence record sets out." .
_:r11b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The Saatchi Art listing of 2014-05-22. One page of the studio's listings on Saatchi Art, the online gallery of the Saatchi Gallery, was captured by the Internet Archive: the listing of the painting Art is Capital under artist account 578159, at 2014-05-22 07:18:32 UTC, titled 'Saatchi Art Artist: Joseph Chirum; Acrylic 2014 Painting \"Art is Capital\"' and described 'Metaconceptual Art Acrylic on Canvas'. It is set out in the record of that work. It carries the term Metaconceptual Art on a third party's site, under a third party's date, seven months before the captures of the studio's own website described above; the listings of the nine other works it links, and the artist's page, were not captured." .
_:r11b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b14 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "artmarketreform.com, attested from February 2014 by Facebook. The studio pointed to a website for the work at artmarketreform.com in two public Facebook comments whose times Facebook assigns. On 2014-02-05 at 07:24:31 UTC, on a photograph posted by the art fair Art Basel, it wrote 'Metaconceptual Art: \"Art Market Reform\" http://artmarketreform.com/'. On 2014-05-09 at 22:15:17 UTC, on a link posted by another page, it wrote 'Because the \"Art Market\" is an unregulated black market ? \"Art Market Reform\" is #Metaconceptual #Art and looks like this: http://artmarketreform.com/'. Both comments carry the address as a link. They show the address in use by the studio, publicly, from February 2014, and the work described there as Metaconceptual Art; they do not show what the site contained, and the Internet Archive holds no capture of artmarketreform.com before 2016. That domain is distinct from ARTMARKETREFORM.ORG, which the registrar's receipts show the studio registering on 2012-11-21. The earliest capture of artmarketreform.com, of 2016-05-09 18:24:02 UTC, is a WordPress blog titled 'Art Market Reform' under the tagline 'All Art History is Art first.', carrying one post, 'History of the Art Market', posted that day under the author name 'Art': a list of fourteen dated press reports on the art market, newest first, from the Wall Street Journal's 'The Price Is Right for Sotheby's' of 8 May 2016 back through the Wildenstein trial, the FBI's warnings on looted antiquities and five reports of 2004 and 2005 on Washington State's collection of use tax on art, to the Sotheby's and Christie's price-fixing case of 2001 and 2002. The studio states, in 2026, that the blog and this post are its own. That is the studio's account; what the capture shows, at the Internet Archive's date, is a site under the work's title compiling the press record of the art market's opacity and taxation, the subject the studio gives Art Market Reform and Art Tax, in the form of the dated press compilation the studio made for its website in 2009. The capture is at https://web.archive.org/web/20160509182402/http://artmarketreform.com/2016/05/09/history-of-the-art-market/; as retrieved on 2026-09-24 it is 22,429 bytes, SHA-256 6eae98ebf0fcf0e5edadb572b2d8e66c753eb3228f570b1b5ca5a1e964dd35d1. The later captures of that year are not attributed to the studio. From 2016-06-24 to 2016-10-13 the address serves a hosting provider's 'account suspended' page, and two posts of 2016-10-08 captured in November, under the same title and tagline, are written in text whose letters are partly replaced by look-alike Cyrillic characters, a device used to disguise reworked or copied text; the studio has not claimed them and this record does not assign them to it. The comments are in the comments file of the account data Facebook compiled on request on 2026-09-24, 874,950 bytes, SHA-256 6bccd7fe8c5e3057c9fccbdcffa62519501e3e8c3585eaccd09eb914ca5249d0, within the archive facebook-joechirum-2026-09-24-TZ87gGfq.zip, SHA-256 c978c3bd338a6a9a934d3aa05a81d2c298c49200bf7e4edc98b8329c02103097." .
_:r11b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b15 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b16 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What is not the studio's, and must not be cited as if it were. The domain wtfisart.com passed out of the studio's hands. A capture of 2016-07-11 shows a hosting suspension page, and from 2020 the domain carries an unrelated publication of art, film and book reviews written under several bylines, which is captured many hundreds of times between 2020 and 2025. The registry record was checked in 2026 and shows a fresh registration of 2024-08-29, so the domain lapsed and was taken up again by someone else. Nothing captured from 2016-07 onward is the studio's work, and the later site's contents say nothing about this collection. A separate incarnation captured on 2016-11-02, a wiki-style 'encyclopedic directory of Art' listing artists, is recorded here only so that a reader who finds it is not misled: it names no work of the studio's and makes no claim this collection relies on." .
_:r11b16 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b16 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b17 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What this record does not show. None of these captures mentions the auction of 30 March 2009, lot 16, Ansel Adams, Sotheby's, or any performance act; the only public statement of that designation on these platforms is the undated Flickr biography. Nothing here bears on the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection. And the 2014 menu item 'Art Market Reform is a happening' is evidence that the studio used the word publicly of a work of its own on a date a third party fixed; it is not evidence of what the studio meant by the word in 2008, which the museum-approach record weighs separately." .
_:r11b17 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b17 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b18 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r11b18 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r11b19 .
_:r11b18 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r11b18 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the archived-websites record" .
_:r11b19 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r11b19 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r11b19 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r11b19 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r11b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: third-party captures, publicly checkable, and the strongest kind of source in this collection short of an institution's own file. The captures are held by the Internet Archive at https://web.archive.org/web/20141218140553/http://wtfisart.com/ , https://web.archive.org/web/20160111165857/http://wtfisart.com/ , https://web.archive.org/web/20160126080729/http://www.sunandrainworks.com/ and https://web.archive.org/web/20160327052433/http://sunandrainworks.com/content/beginnings-art-odyssey . The capture timestamps are the Archive's own and are not the studio's to set; that is what makes them evidence. Two things inside the pages are not: the content management system's 'Submitted by' stamps, which a site owner can set or change, and the page text itself, which is the studio's writing. So the dates to cite are the capture dates, and the stamps are reported as what the site said of itself." .
_:r11b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the wtfisart.com captures show, and how little it is. The earliest capture of the domain, 2010-01-25, is not a page: it is a redirect, sent by a web server that named itself as serving wtfisart.com, to a generic page on securepaynet.net, a domain of the registrar's hosting service, so a hosting account existed but served no studio content. The next, 2011-02-10, is a parked advertising page with no studio content. Neither says anything about what the site carried in 2009: the registrar's receipts record shows the domain was registered for one year on 2009-03-16 and not renewed in the year that followed, so by the 2011 capture it had lapsed, and registrars commonly park a lapsed address. The studio registered the name again on 2011-10-16, eight months after the 2011 capture, which is why the captures below resume. On 2013-07-20 the domain carries one line, 'Under Construction:Check back soon !', on a page the studio wrote itself; the passage below sets out how that is told apart from the parked captures around it. By 2014-12-18, and still on 2016-01-11, it carries a Drupal installation whose main menu reads 'Artists', 'Art Market Reform is a happening' and 'Got Provenance ?'. The body text is the same paragraph repeated in three panels, ending 'For the Love of God, We are the Art. Long Live the Art Spirit !', and the page states 'Welcome to wtfisart.com. No front page content has been created yet.' That last sentence should be quoted by anyone citing this record. What was captured is a demonstration installation carrying the studio's slogans in its menu, not a working publication. Its value is narrow and real: on a date fixed by a third party, the studio was publicly calling a work of its own a happening, and was publishing the line that is handwritten on the Art Notes board." .
_:r11b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The slider on the 2014 site carries three of the studio's paintings. The site of 2014-12-18, and unchanged on 2016-01-11, runs Drupal under a theme called CorporateClean, and its three slider images are requested from sites/all/themes/corporateclean/mockup/, which is where that theme ships its own sample pictures. The files served from those paths are not the theme's samples. They are photographs of paintings: slide-1 carries the words 'Art is Capital', slide-2 'ART MARKET REFORM', and slide-3 'what hedge funds ?', each in painted letters across a worked ground. All three are archived and can be fetched: 81,058, 93,694 and 86,711 bytes, each 500 by 250 pixels. Above them the slider headings read 'Art Market Reform is a happening', 'Art is Capital invest with confidence' and 'What Hedge Funds ?! invest in Art'. So three paintings are dated to the studio's own site by a third party, twice, thirteen months apart." .
_:r11b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The Art Market Reform on the site is not the one published here. The painting in slide-2 is lettered across a blue ground worked with white, and the painting published with this collection's Art Market Reform record is a different object: the words set over a dense multicoloured ground, with 'Art' in block letters and 'Market Reform' in script. They are two paintings sharing a title, not two photographs of one painting. The studio has said its works exist and are held in its collection; this capture shows that at least one title names more than one object, which any fuller record of these works will have to describe as a set." .
_:r11b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the slider does not establish, stated so the capture is not asked to carry more than it can. The three images are 500 by 250 pixels, a banner shape, so each is a crop and none documents a whole painting. The capture dates the photographs' presence on the site and nothing about when the paintings were made. The same page carries Drupal's untouched message that no front page content has been created yet, so the installation was only partly configured, and the slider headings sit above the theme's own placeholder paragraph repeated three times. By the capture of 2016-10-09 the site had been rebuilt on Open Atrium and the slider was gone." .
_:r11b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The phrase that runs through the site, and the studio's account of it. The body text of the 2014 and 2016 captures ends 'For the Love of God, We are the Art. Long Live the Art Spirit !'. The studio states, in 2026, that this was by design: that the sign-off 'For the Love of Art', which closes its approaches of August 2008 and its note returning the signed consignment agreement in March 2009, and the line 'For the love of God, We are th[...]!' on the Art Notes panel, and this sentence on the website, are one chain rather than three separate flourishes. The objective record is consistent with that and does not establish it: the sign-off is documented in the correspondence of 2008 and 2009, the panel line is photographed in a file dated 2012, and this is the phrase's earliest appearance in a third-party capture. The line itself is dated earlier by Facebook, closing a private message of 2012-10-30, 'For The Love of God, We Are the Art.', as set out in the record of the 2012-2013 Facebook and USA Projects evidence. Which came first in intention is the studio's statement, not the documents'. The studio further states that 'Long Live the Art Spirit' comes from Robert Henri's The Art Spirit, the collection of his teaching published in 1923, which it says it read around 2006. Nothing here dates that reading, and the phrase's earliest dated appearance in this collection is this capture of 2014-12-18." .
_:r11b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The capture of 2013-07-20 is not a parked page, and the captures on either side of it are. This matters more than the one line of text it carries, so the three are set out together as the archive serves them. On 2011-02-10 the address answers with a frameset pointing at sedoparking.com, a domain-parking service. On 2014-01-05 it answers with a single full-page iframe pointing at a GoDaddy parking path. Between them, on 2013-07-20, it answers with eleven lines of hand-written HTML: a doctype, a title reading 'Under Construction:Check back soon !', a background colour set inline to #00ccff, and one image tag for underconstruction.png at 842 by 443 pixels. There is no advertising frame, no registrar iframe and no third-party script, and the image is requested by a relative path, so it was being served from the domain itself rather than from a parking provider. Whoever held the address in July 2013 had put their own page on it. That is a stronger statement than the earlier reading of this capture, which took it for a bare holding page and did not distinguish it from the parked ones." .
_:r11b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What that capture still does not establish, which is most of what one would want. The page carries no name, no studio, no mention of art and no link: nothing in it identifies its author. The inference that it is the studio's rests on the registration rather than on the page, because the receipts record has the studio registering wtfisart.com again on 2011-10-16, and the address is serving hand-made content within that later registration rather than within the lapse that preceded it. The studio's own account, given in 2026, is that the page was its own and in its own style of the time. That account is recorded as the studio's; the page cannot confirm it. The image the page displayed was never captured: a query for underconstruction.png returns no result at any timestamp, so the only picture on the page is the one thing about it that cannot be examined. And the parked capture of 2014-01-05 shows that whatever was being built in July 2013 was not being served six months later, which is worth keeping in view beside the Drupal site that appears at the end of that year." .
_:r11b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: the executed Sotheby's consignment agreement (March 2009)" .
_:r12b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r12b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r12b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r12b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-consignment-agreement-2009" .
_:r12b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r12b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r12b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The name of the file. The signed agreement was returned to Sotheby's on 2009-03-19 as an attachment called 'JoeArtChirum.pdf'. Read as the three-part personal name its form suggests, that is the consignor's given name, then the word Art where a middle name belongs, then his surname. His actual middle name, which appears on the identity page bound into this same file and is not reproduced here, is not Art. So the word has been substituted into his own name, deliberately, in the filename of the executed consignment contract, and sent under that name to the auction house. The covering message that carried the file is signed 'Joe M. Chirum', with his real middle initial. So in the same message the consignor signed himself by his ordinary name and named the attached contract with Art in that name's middle place; the substitution was made for the filename, not carried over from a habitual way of signing. This is a different kind of thing from the art-inflected phrases that run through the correspondence. Those are turns of phrase inside messages about appraisal, shipping and file formats. This is an act of self-naming, chosen rather than written in passing, and attached to the single most consequential document of the engagement at the moment of executing it. Alongside 'this happening' in the museum letter of 2008-08-11, it is the strongest contemporaneous indication in this collection that the person carrying out the consignment understood himself to be doing something artistic while doing it." .
_:r12b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "How far the filename can be taken, and how far it cannot. It is evidence of self-conception, not of designation. A person may sign himself as an artist in the act of consigning a photograph without thereby making the consignment a work, and nothing in the file, the agreement, or the covering message says that the auction is a work. The reading also has an ordinary competitor: a jaunty filename from a correspondent who elsewhere signs off 'Yours in Art', chosen to be memorable rather than to mean anything. Against that competitor stands the placement, since the substitution falls exactly where a middle name falls and nowhere else, and the moment, since this is the contract rather than a covering note. The studio's own account, recorded in the correspondence record, is that the form of the work required the counterparty not to be told; a signature concealed in a filename is consistent with that account, and consistency is not proof of it. This record states the fact of the substitution and both readings, and chooses neither." .
_:r12b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r12b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r12b13 .
_:r12b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r12b12 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the consignment agreement record" .
_:r12b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r12b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r12b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r12b13 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r12b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Why this document is not published, first reason: it is contractually confidential. The agreement contains an undertaking by the consignor not to disclose its terms to any third party without Sotheby's prior written consent, excepting attorneys and accountants on a need-to-know basis and disclosure compelled by legal process. That undertaking binds the studio now. Accordingly this record states only what the document is, when it was made, who the parties were, and that it was signed by both of them. It gives no commission rate, fee, charge, settlement term, estimate, or reserve drawn from the agreement, and no account, consignment, or licence number appearing on its face. A reader should not infer that those terms are unremarkable or otherwise; the studio has simply not read them into the public record, and would need Sotheby's written consent to do so." .
_:r12b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Why this document is not published, second reason: one page of it is a photocopy of a government-issued identity document, supplied for the identity verification the agreement requires. It carries a full legal name, date of birth, licence number, a residential address, physical description, and a photograph. That page will not be published in any form, redacted or otherwise, and the file as a whole is excluded from this repository by rule rather than by care, so that it cannot be committed by accident. The identifiers on it are held in the studio's local list of terms that the commit guard blocks." .
_:r12b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The file as held, and what its metadata says. 'JoeArtChirum.pdf', 555,754 bytes, SHA-256 970c8e85bf0931ac8bfed1ab742d7f67247cdb2003c4db4aea99b9b29138eabe. Seven pages, none carrying a text layer: page one is a JPEG-compressed scan at 2548 by 3504 pixels, pages two to seven are bilevel CCITT Group 4 scans at 1274 by 1752. The document information dictionary gives a creator of Adobe Acrobat 7.08 and a producer of its Image Conversion Plug-in, which is what converts scanned images into a PDF. Its creation and modification dates are identical, D:20090319130724-07'00', that is 2009-03-19 at 13:07:24 with a stated offset of seven hours behind UTC. The individual page objects carry their own creation stamps of 13:05:43 and 13:05:44, ninety seconds before the document was assembled, which is the ordinary signature of pages scanned in one pass and then combined. The checksum is published so the held file can later be shown to be the one described here without the file itself being disclosed, following the convention used for the 2013 museum correspondence." .
_:r12b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "An apparent discrepancy, recorded rather than smoothed over. The PDF states its creation as 13:07:24 at an offset of UTC minus seven, which is 20:07:24 UTC. The mail client displaying the message that carried it shows a send time of 12:16 in the afternoon of the same day. If that display were in Pacific daylight time, also UTC minus seven, the message would predate its own attachment by fifty-one minutes, which is impossible. The discrepancy is one of rendering, not of substance: a mail client shows times in the timezone configured for the account or the machine viewing it, not the timezone of the sender in 2009. Any setting of UTC minus eight or further west resolves it. The setting has since been observed rather than assumed. The same mailbox's client displays the museum letter of 2008-08-11 as sent at 3:01 PM, although that message's raw Date line reads 16:01:02 at UTC minus seven, which is 23:01 UTC; that is a rendering at UTC minus eight. At the same offset the 12:16 display here is 20:16 UTC, nine minutes after the file was made, which is the order the documents require. Both screenshots were taken in the same mail client, as the studio confirmed on 2026-09-10; that its timezone setting was unchanged between the two captures is assumed rather than shown. The display describes the viewer's settings, not the sender, so it says nothing about where the message was sent from. The point is noted because an auditor comparing the two figures will find it." .
_:r12b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The message that carried it, and an artifact inside it. The Photographs department's message of 2009-03-18 is held as a screenshot. It states, in capitals, that it is imperative the agreement be signed and returned as soon as possible, and that a signed agreement is required before the photograph can be sold; the word 'agreement' is misspelled in the original. It speaks of 'the sale of your photograph on March 30, 2009', the auction house's own statement of the sale date in the message carrying the contract. It offers three ways to return it, 'by PDF in an email, by fax, or by FedEx or UPS', so the electronic return the studio made the following day was the first of the three the auction house itself proposed, and not an expedient of the studio's own. A second screenshot, of that return as it sits in the studio's sent mail, quotes the department's message beneath it with its raw header lines, giving its Date as 'Wed, 18 Mar 2009 18:36:10 -0400', which is 22:36:10 UTC; the signed file was assembled the next day at 20:07:24 UTC, in the order the exchange requires. The same view lists the attachment as 'JoeArtChirum.pdf' at 543 KB. The held file is 555,754 bytes, which is 542.7 kibibytes and displays as 543 KB. That agreement of name and size is consistent with the held file being the one attached; a displayed size is not a checksum, and it does not prove it. Below the department's signature the message carries a forwarded header from an internal address at the same institution, under the subject 'Message from KMBT_C352', which is the model designation of a Konica Minolta multifunction scanner: the agreement was scanned on the auction house's own device and mailed onward from it. That header displays a send time of 7:42 PM, recorded here as a rendering and not as a fact about New York, for the reason given above about displayed times. The studio's mailbox in 2009 was a Hotmail account, as the provider's promotional footer on the message shows, whereas the 2013 museum correspondence sits with a different provider; that bears on any future export of these messages as files with their headers intact." .
_:r12b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What it settles. This collection recorded until now that the identity of the consignor of record was not established, because the published catalogue names no consignor. The consignment correspondence narrowed that; this closes it. A consignment agreement is the instrument by which a consignor consigns property for sale, and here it exists, is dated eleven days before the sale, names the studio principal, and is signed by him and countersigned for the auction house. Two limits still hold and are not affected. The catalogue itself remains silent, so a reader working only from public sources cannot see this; every statement elsewhere in this collection that the catalogue does not name Sun & Rain Works stays correct. The agreement is made in the studio principal's own name rather than the business name, which is what the business form requires and not a separation from it: the opening approach of 2008-08-14 told Sotheby's that its writer was 'the sole proprietor of a fledgeling Art business, Sun and Rain Works', and a sole proprietorship has no legal personality distinct from its proprietor, so it contracts in his name. On the ordinary understanding of that business form, the consignor named on this agreement and the business disclosed to the auction house at first contact are the same legal person. This record states that as the general position for a sole proprietorship rather than as advice on this contract, which is governed by New York law and has not been reviewed by a lawyer for this collection. A second document from the auction house's side now agrees with this one: its consignor statement for sale N08533, supplied to the studio as a duplicate on 2026-09-25, is issued under a Sotheby's client account in the name of 'Mr Joseph Chirum' and records lot 16 as sold. It is described, with its checksum, in the auction-house correspondence record. In the same reply the auction house said it could not locate its own copy of this agreement, so the studio's copy is the only one known." .
_:r12b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What it does not touch. The agreement is a contract for the sale of property at auction and contains nothing about art as a concept, a performance, a designation, or a project. The covering message that returned it carries two art-inflected phrases, a remark that an exception to saving paper is acceptable for the purposes of Art, and a sign-off reading 'Yours in Art'. Both are turns of phrase in a note about file formats. With this exchange the Sotheby's thread reaches eleven messages across seven months, from first approach to executed contract, and no message among them states that the consignment or the sale is an artwork. An earlier version of this passage gave the count as eight and said that nothing in the thread frames the consignment as an artwork. The first was stale and the second is withdrawn as too strong, for the reasons the correspondence record sets out: what is absent across the thread is a designation, not a vocabulary, the studio's own language in it being persistently art-inflected. That silence is not fatal to the designation this collection records, which is dated to 2013 and evidenced there, and the weight of that silence should not be overstated. Trade correspondence with a cataloguer and an administrator, about estimates, deadlines, shipping and an unreturned signature, is not where an artist would set out a conceptual framing, and the absence of one there is weak evidence about intention. It is worth more that the only letter of this period addressed to an art institution rather than to the trade, the approach to a museum photography department of 2008-08-11, is also the one place in the whole correspondence where a conceptual reading is available. What remains true, and is the actual gap, is that no document of 2008 or 2009 designates the auction as a work; the earliest that does is of 2013." .
_:r12b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Redaction and consent. The Sotheby's staff member who sent and chased the agreement is identified by department and role only; the principal auctioneer named on the document's face is not named here either. Their names, the institutional telephone and mailbox details, the studio principal's addresses, and every account, consignment, licence and identity number appearing in the file are withheld and retained in the studio's private copy. Sotheby's holds its own executed counterpart of this agreement. Any publication of the document or of its terms would require that institution's written consent, which has not been sought at the date of this record." .
_:r12b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> .
_:r13b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P75i_is_possessed_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r13b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E30_Right> .
_:r13b0 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "CC BY 4.0" .
_:r13b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: documentation photographs of the Ansel Adams print (August 2008)" .
_:r13b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r13b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r13b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r13b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A correction to this record, about the date of that frame. An earlier version of this passage said the frame carried no reliable date, that a filesystem timestamp of 2009-01-20 was all that remained, and that no date was asserted for the series. That was wrong on every count, and it was written without consulting the record that already covers these files. The staged-photographs record dates the series to autumn 2008 by a three-part analysis which is stronger than anything offered here. Its floor is independent of any clock: the files were rewritten by QuickTime 7.5.5 running on Mac OS X 10.5.5, released on 2008-09-09 and 2008-09-15, so they cannot have been processed by that pairing before the middle of September 2008. Its middle term is the processing time those rewrites left, 2008-11-25 between 18:56:10 and 18:56:17 in frame order. And the 2009-01-20 timestamp cited in the earlier version of this passage is in that record too, correctly, as the ceiling rather than the date: it is the file system's record of the copy into the studio's working folder, not of the capture or the processing. That record also explains the file names, which this one did not: an Olympus that has never had its clock set reports 1 January, and builds 'P101' out of it. The date of capture itself remains unestablished, as it says. Nothing in that analysis is displaced by the copies from which the published frame was taken, whose own timestamps are a property of the copying and carry no weight." .
_:r13b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r13b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r13b12 .
_:r13b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r13b11 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the 2008 documentation-photograph evidence record" .
_:r13b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r13b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r13b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r13b12 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r13b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-consignment-photographs-2008" .
_:r13b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r13b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r13b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: independently checkable. Unlike the studio's correspondence and receipts, this record can be tested by any third party without access to private material. The public Sotheby's catalogue entry for sale N08533, lot 16, at https://www.sothebys.com/en/auctions/ecatalogue/2009/photographs-n08533/lot.16.html describes the print as carrying the photographer's 'Photograph by Ansel Adams for The American Trust Company' stamp, as numbered 1-SF-46, as titled, and as annotated 'Wells Fargo Bank' in ink on the reverse by the photographer; the catalogue note explains the annotation as Adams's own correction following the 1960 merger of The American Trust Company into Wells Fargo Bank. Each of those particulars is visible in the studio's 2008 verso photograph, which is published with this record, except that the negative number is legible only as far as '1-SF-' before the right edge of the frame cuts it off; the catalogue's '46' is therefore not visible and is not claimed here. A vertical crease runs through the mount to the left of the number but does not obscure it. The correspondence is therefore in four particulars - the credit stamp, the negative-number series, the inked Wells Fargo annotation over the struck-through article, and the handwritten title - and is not dependent on the studio's own account." .
_:r13b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Embedded image metadata, as recorded by the camera: all eleven files carry Exif written at capture by a FUJIFILM FinePix1300, reporting the firmware string 'Digital Camera FinePix1300 Ver1.24'. Exif DateTimeOriginal values run from 2008-08-08 00:05:33 to 2008-08-08 00:10:22, a continuous session of under five minutes. The frames are numbered DSCF0012 to DSCF0017 and DSCF0020 to DSCF0024; frames 0018 and 0019 are absent from the sequence. This metadata is written by the camera at exposure and is independent of the filesystem timestamps of the storage medium, which agree with it. It remains studio-held and is not notarized: no checksums, file hashes, or custody identifiers are published at this time, and Exif fields can in principle be altered." .
_:r13b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Contemporaneous conceptual intent. One frame in the sequence, exposed at 2008-08-08 00:08:46, shows the print photographed together with a copy of 'The Philosophy of Andy Warhol (From A to B and Back Again)', stood upright and facing the camera on the same ground. Sun & Rain Works reads this juxtaposition as a deliberate art-referential gesture made at the time of documentation, and as the earliest dated trace of the thinking behind the concept of the auction as artwork. The scope of that reading should be stated plainly: the photograph evidences that the two objects were deliberately composed in one frame in August 2008. It is not a written designation, it does not by itself establish that the auction engagement was framed as art in 2008 or 2009, and the conceptual designation remains an assertion by the artist made explicit over time." .
_:r13b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Limits of the correspondence. Ansel Adams produced multiple prints from the 1952 negative, so agreement on the verso markings identifies a print of the same issue and annotation state rather than establishing a unique object beyond question; the strength of the match rests on the combination of particulars, not on any one of them. The photographs show that the print was in the room in which they were taken; the attribution of that room and camera to Sun & Rain Works rests on the studio's custody of the storage medium. The Sotheby's catalogue names no consignor, as is standard auction practice, so the identity of the consignor of record is not established by these photographs. It is evidenced separately: the consignment correspondence record carries two messages of March 2009 in which the auction house's own Photographs department writes to the studio principal about 'your photograph' and chases the signed consignment agreement it had posted in February. Since then the signed agreement has been recovered, and the auction house's own sale statement for the lot, supplied in 2026, names the studio principal as its consignor." .
_:r13b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Corroborated from outside the files. The eleven frames described here were transmitted as a zip attachment to Sotheby's on 2008-08-14, six days after exposure, and that attachment is held: its twelve entries are byte-for-byte identical to the studio's loose copies, and its Info-ZIP UNIX extra fields carry Unix-epoch modification times of 2008-08-08 07:05:36Z to 07:10:24Z. The covering message describes the photographs as made with a 1.3 megapixel FujiFilm camera, which agrees with the FinePix1300 named in the Exif and with the 1280 by 960 frame size. The caveat recorded above, that Exif fields can in principle be altered, should now be read against that agreement. One frame in the set, DSCF0020, is the frame composing the print with Warhol's philosophy; it was among those sent. See the 2008 approach record." .
_:r13b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The eleven frames are also held as loose files, and their two clocks agree. Besides the archive San Francisco.zip, the studio holds the eleven photographs as individual files carrying filesystem timestamps. Those timestamps run from 23:05:36 to 23:10:24 on 2008-08-07, while the EXIF DateTimeOriginal values of the same files run from 00:05:33 to 00:10:22 on 2008-08-08. The difference is constant, between fifty-nine minutes fifty-seven seconds and fifty-nine minutes fifty-eight seconds across the set, and the intervals between successive frames are the same in both series. This does not re-date the session, and an earlier reading of it that did has been discarded. Every filesystem timestamp falls on an even second, which is the two-second granularity of the FAT file system used by camera cards, and FAT stores local time with no timezone, so the same stored value is read an hour out when the reading system applies different daylight-saving rules. A one-hour offset between a FAT timestamp and an EXIF value is therefore the expected artefact of reading the card later and elsewhere, not evidence that either clock was wrong. What the agreement does establish is worth having on its own. The two series track each other frame for frame, so the session was a single unbroken run of about five minutes rather than an assembly from several occasions; and the files carry card timestamps at all, which means they were preserved from the camera card rather than re-saved by later software. The session date this collection uses remains the EXIF date, 2008-08-08, which is the camera's own record of its own exposure." .
_:r13b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A second photograph of the verso, from a different camera, showing the whole inscription. The close-up published with this record as images/adams-verso-2008.jpg is a frame from the session of 2008-08-08, and it is cropped: it carries the credit block and the inked 'Wells Fargo Bank' annotation, but its right edge cuts off the negative number after '1-SF-' and cuts the handwritten title off after 'San Francisco fr'. The studio also holds a photograph of the same verso made with a different camera, an Olympus C3100Z, in which the whole inscription is within the frame and legible: the printed block 'PHOTOGRAPH BY ANSEL ADAMS FOR THE AMERICAN TRUST COMPANY' with 'THE' struck through and 'Wells Fargo Bank' written in by hand, an empty 'NO.' line, 'NEGATIVE No. 1-SF-46', and beneath it, in the same hand, 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks'. It is published here as images/adams-verso-full-2008.jpg. Every element it shows is already independently attested by the public auction catalogue entry for lot 16 of sale N08533, which records the stamp, the number 1-SF-46, the title and the Wells Fargo annotation; the photograph adds legibility, not a new fact. That frame is not a stray file. It is one of a series of seventeen already recorded in this collection as the staged photographs of the print, where all seventeen are enumerated and the two verso frames are noted; what is new here is that one of them is published, so the whole inscription can be read rather than described." .
_:r13b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
