_:r1b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r1b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "work:art-market-reform" .
_:r1b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r1b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r1b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A visual anchor for the project: art, price, reform, and institutional display held together as one unstable field." .
_:r1b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r1b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r1b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The work exists as an object, and the image published here is a photograph of it. The studio states, on 2026-09-22, that Art Market Reform exists and is held in its own collection, and that the file published with this record is a photograph of that physical work rather than a born-digital image. That is the studio's statement about its own holdings, recorded as such; nobody outside the studio has examined the object, and no measurement, medium, support or date of making is recorded here because none has been supplied. Two consequences should be read off it. The first is that this record's modelling is provisional and known to be incomplete: it types the work as a digital object classified as a digital image, which describes the photograph and not the thing photographed, and a fuller record would describe the object and treat this file as documentation of it. That correction is deferred until the object's particulars are to hand, and is noted here so that a reader does not take the present typing for a considered claim about what the work is. The second is that the photograph's own date is not established: the file as published carries no camera metadata, having been re-encoded, so it dates neither the object nor the photographing." .
_:r1b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r1b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r1b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A second painting carries this title, and it is the one the studio put on its website. The Internet Archive's capture of the studio's site on 2014-12-18, and again on 2016-01-11, carries a photograph of a painting lettered 'ART MARKET REFORM' across a blue ground worked with white. That is not the painting photographed for this record, which sets the words over a dense multicoloured ground with 'Art' in block letters and 'Market Reform' in script. Two paintings therefore share the title, and the one a third party dates is not the one published here. The consequence for this record is that its subject is ambiguous as it stands: it names a title, publishes a photograph of one object, and does not say which object the title's other uses in this collection refer to. A fuller record should describe a set and say which painting each statement is about." .
_:r1b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r1b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r1b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A website for the work, 2014. In public Facebook comments of 2014-02-05 and 2014-05-09, at times Facebook assigns, the studio linked artmarketreform.com as the work's site, the second reading '\"Art Market Reform\" is #Metaconceptual #Art and looks like this: http://artmarketreform.com/'. No capture of that site from 2014 exists, so what it showed then is not known. The earliest capture, of 2016-05-09, shows a blog titled 'Art Market Reform' with a single post, 'History of the Art Market', a dated compilation of press reports on the art market's opacity and taxation, which the studio states in 2026 was its own; the comments are set out in the archived-websites record." .
_:r1b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r1b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r1b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Named, and called a title, in a letter to a museum on 2013-09-12. The studio's letter to the Jordan Schnitzer Museum of Art introduces two works by name: 'Two popular examples of Metaconceptual Art, which are ongoing through time and space as well, are titled \"Art Market Reform\" , and \"Art Tax\"'. It then glosses this one: 'Promoting Art Tax on the local, state, national, and international levels to encourage and promote accountability, transparency, and regulation in the Art Market and Art World.' That is the earliest naming of this work delivered to an institution, though not the earliest dated: a public Facebook comment of 2012-08-14 reads 'Coming Soon: Art Market Reform ! ! !', and a private message of 2013-05-29 calls it one of the writer's more recent works of Metaconceptual Art, both set out in the record of the 2012-2013 Facebook and USA Projects evidence; it is in the artist's own words, and it was delivered to a named institution which produced its own copy from its archive in 2026, so the date does not rest on the studio's mailbox alone. Three limits go with it. The letter attests that the title was in use on that date; it does not date the making of the image published with this record, and its own phrase, that the works are 'ongoing through time and space', describes them as continuing rather than finished. The letter attaches images but names none of them, so nothing in it identifies the file published here as the thing it names. And a title used in 2013 says nothing about 2009 or before." .
_:r1b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r1b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r1b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r1b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r1b10 .
_:r1b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r1b9 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Creation of Art Market Reform" .
_:r20b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: a photograph of two studio works inscribed 'W.T.F. is Art ?' (8 November 2011)" .
_:r20b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r20b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r20b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r20b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-inscription-photograph-2011" .
_:r20b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r20b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r20b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What this record does not show. It shows that the two works, and the inscription 'W.T.F. is Art ?', existed on or before 2011-11-08, by the file's own account. It does not date either work's making. It does not show that anyone outside the studio saw either physical work, or this photograph, at any date; the board's words reached a public platform in 2013 through the digital work, and the sheet's inscription is not shown to have reached one at all. It says nothing about what was served at wtfisart.com at any date, nothing about the 2009 auction designation, and nothing about the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection. It carries no statement that the phrase declares a work of art." .
_:r20b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r20b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r20b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r20b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r20b12 .
_:r20b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r20b11 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the inscription photograph record" .
_:r20b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r20b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r20b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r20b12 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r20b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: held by the studio; the file's own account, unusually complete. The file is the camera's original: a JPEG of 2,048 by 1,536 pixels with its Exif block intact, including the camera's 400 by 300 embedded thumbnail, which shows the same scene. It is held as 1108111218.JPEG, 487,576 bytes, SHA-256 469087b91ba1d18fb7beaa8f2d3bbec61959ea1ad57e9b1e2d3f645b29d73a00, and is published here at /images/wtf-is-art-sheet-2011.jpg as a byte-identical copy of that file, renamed for the web and not otherwise altered, so the checksum describes the published image as well. No person, face, vehicle plate or address is visible in it." .
_:r20b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r20b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r20b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "How the photograph is dated, and how firmly. The Exif block names the camera as an LG Electronics LG-VX8575, a camera phone sold on the Verizon Wireless network, with firmware VX857V06, and gives DateTimeOriginal and DateTimeDigitized both as 2011:11:08 12:18:15. The field records no timezone and no location; the file carries no GPS data. The file name, 1108111218, is the phone's own naming, built from the month, day, two-digit year, hour and minute of capture, and it gives the same minute independently of the Exif field. The Software field names only the phone's firmware, and no editing application has written to the file. Two cautions go with this. Exif fields and file names can be rewritten, so the date remains the file's own account and is not notarized. And a phone of this kind ordinarily takes its clock from the carrier network rather than from its owner, which makes an accidental misdating less likely than with a camera whose clock is set by hand; it does not rule out a deliberate one. The file-system dates of the copy now held are of 2026 and say nothing about 2011." .
_:r20b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r20b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r20b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The inscription, read closely. The words on the sheet run up its right-hand edge, rotated a quarter turn, in small dark capitals and lower case: an opening quotation mark, 'W.T.F.' with a full stop after each letter, 'is Art', a space, a question mark, and a closing quotation mark. They are legible when that part of the image is enlarged and turned upright; most of the surrounding notes on the sheet are not legible at this resolution and are not transcribed here. The inscription is punctuated as a question. That bears on the reading of the domain name set out in the hosting-receipts record, where the studio holds that wtfisart was chosen to read both as a question and as a statement. This photograph shows the studio writing the phrase as a question, in 2011; it is evidence that the question reading was the studio's own, and it is not evidence for the statement reading." .
_:r20b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r20b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r20b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The board's phrase in the studio's letter of 2013. On 2013-09-12 the studio wrote to a university art museum, in the message the museum-correspondence record describes, that 'together we are helping the Art Historians of the future to keep their employment, here and now'. The board in this photograph carries the same words and the same closing 'here and now', and by the photograph's date it existed twenty-two months before that letter was sent. The letter is retained in the museum's own archive; the photograph is held by the studio. The agreement between them shows the phrase was the studio's own and in use before 2013. It does not date the board's making, which could be earlier than November 2011 but is not shown to be." .
_:r20b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r20b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r20b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Where the photograph falls among the domain's dates. The registrar's receipts give WTFISART.COM as first registered on 2009-03-16, lapsed by 2011-02-10, when the Internet Archive recorded a parked page, and registered again on 2011-10-16. This photograph was taken twenty-three days after that re-registration. It shows the studio working with the phrase in its physical work at the time it took the name back. It is not evidence of anything in 2009, and it does not show the phrase in any public place in 2011." .
_:r20b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r20b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r20b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The digital work made from the board, posted 2013-06-02. The studio's Flickr account, at flickr.com/photos/24377024@N07/8920785186/, carries a public photograph titled 'ArtHistoriansOTheFuture' with the description 'MetaConceptual Art'. Flickr records it as posted at 2013-06-02 05:55:56 UTC, which is the evening of 1 June 2013 on the American west coast. It is the board's inscription, cropped to the lettering and recoloured over a saturated rainbow ground: the same words, line breaks and letterforms as the board in the photograph of 2011, down to the tilde and closing parenthesis of '(Here and Now. ~)'. The studio states that the physical board was the foundation of this digital work. The studio's held copy, 8920785186_4cfd9c6af9_b.jpg, 1,022 by 285 pixels and 89,727 bytes, SHA-256 2e185a7bfa5e200aee5377ec2c7c523548e0de63d01aa27ac059463b7b82104f, was compared on 2026-09-15 with the file Flickr serves at that size and is byte-identical to it; Flickr also serves the image at up to 1,600 by 446 pixels. That held copy is published here at /images/art-historians-of-the-future-2013.jpg, renamed and otherwise unaltered, so the checksum describes the published image, the studio's held copy and the file Flickr serves alike. The file carries no Exif, and Flickr's 'taken' time of 2013-06-01 22:55:47, nine seconds before the posting, is the value it supplies for a file without one, as the archived-websites record sets out for the Art Notes photograph posted the same day. The posting date is assigned by the platform and is the evidence; the page recorded eleven views when checked. This places the board's words in public, on a third party's platform, three months before the museum letter. It does not date the digital work's making beyond that, and it does not carry the phrase W.T.F. is Art, which is on the sheet and not on the board." .
_:r20b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r20b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r20b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Two works carry the line, a year apart. The sheet photographed here is the second work in this collection inscribed with the phrase. The first is the notice board published as Art Notes, which carries 'WTF is Art ?!' beside the name 'Sun and Rain Works', and which is photographed in a file dated 30 November 2012 and posted publicly to the studio's Flickr account on 2013-06-02, the same day as the digital work described above. This photograph is dated 2011-11-08, nearly a year earlier than the Art Notes file, and the two inscriptions are not the same object: they are written in different hands of the same writer, on different supports, and punctuated differently, 'W.T.F. is Art ?' here and 'WTF is Art ?!' there. The phrase therefore recurs across separate works of the studio rather than appearing once. What that carries is modest but real: the words were in continuous use in the studio's own work between the domain's first registration in 2009 and its public postings of 2013, and neither occurrence depends on the other for its date. Neither is a dated public act in itself; the Art Notes board reached a public platform in 2013, and this sheet is not shown to have reached one at all." .
_:r20b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r20b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r20b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The titles, and the survival of the works, are the studio's account. Neither title, 'Helping the Art Historians of the future' or 'Dancing with Mummies', is written on either work in any part visible in the photograph; the board carries its phrase in quotation marks but not as a labelled title. The identification of the sheet's title, and its reference to Thomas Hoving, whose memoir of his directorship of the Metropolitan Museum of Art is titled Making the Mummies Dance, are given by the studio in 2026. The studio states that neither physical work survives. The sheet is therefore known only from this photograph, and the board from this photograph and the digital work made from it. When each physical work was made is not established by this record." .
_:r20b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r20b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r21b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> .
_:r21b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P75i_is_possessed_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r21b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E30_Right> .
_:r21b0 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "CC BY 4.0" .
_:r21b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: Sun & Rain Works auction engagement (2009)" .
_:r21b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r21b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r21b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r21b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-live-auction-2009" .
_:r21b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r21b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r21b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: mixed. Independently verifiable: (1) the public Sotheby's catalogue lot (sale N08533, lot 16) at https://www.sothebys.com/en/auctions/ecatalogue/2009/photographs-n08533/lot.16.html ; (2) two Sun and Rain Works public posts carrying the 'WTF is Art' theme, both still live on the account when checked on 2026-09-10, and each dated independently by the platform's timestamp-encoded identifier. The earlier reads '#WTF is #Art ?!?!?!' (X post 372900008367894529). Its identifier decodes to 2013-08-29T01:54:24Z, and the platform's own embed gives August 29, 2013. The phrase itself is older in public: a Facebook comment by the studio principal of 2012-11-21, on a post by a New York art critic, reads '#WTF is #Art ?! give thanks !', at a time Facebook assigns, and is set out in the record of the 2012-2013 Facebook and USA Projects evidence. The studio's screenshot of the post shows Aug 28, 2013, because a displayed date is rendered in the viewer's timezone, and 01:54 UTC on 29 August is still 28 August in every timezone from UTC minus two westward, which includes all of the Americas. The later reads '#WTF is #Art ?!' (X post 373257603108704256). Its identifier decodes to 2013-08-30T01:35:22Z, and the embed gives August 30, 2013. The two were posted 23 hours and 41 minutes apart. Neither is assigned a local day here: nothing in this collection places the studio in a particular timezone in 2013. An earlier version of this passage dated the second post to 2013-08-29 by taking Hawaii-Aleutian time as the studio's local time; that assumption is withdrawn. Held privately by the studio: the 2008-2009 correspondence, receipts, and photographs. Selected private items can be promoted to public evidence as redaction and rights review are completed." .
_:r21b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r21b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r21b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The consignment engagement is corroborated by inbound 2008 correspondence from Sotheby's Photographs (confirming intake logistics and naming the department administrator) and by a shared Hana, HI mailing address linking the consignor to the Sun & Rain Works domain receipts. Contemporaneous conceptual intent is evidenced by art-referential documentation (an Andy Warhol volume juxtaposed with the photograph in images sent to Sotheby's). Each item is scoped to what it shows: these evidence identity, consignment, intent, and the concept's public continuity — not a witnessed live-creation event, and not precedence over any later artist or event." .
_:r21b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r21b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r21b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Part of the documentation described here has since been dated and published as a record in its own right. The art-referential documentation photographs — including the frame composing the print with a volume of Warhol's philosophy — carry camera-written Exif placing them on 2008-08-08, and their close-up of the print's verso agrees in four particulars with the public Sotheby's catalogue entry for lot 16. That material is no longer only studio-reported: see the documentation-photograph evidence record." .
_:r21b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r21b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r21b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The catalogue entry, captured and checked, and a gap that remains open. This collection calls the public catalogue entry for lot 16 its independently verifiable anchor, and thirteen records rely on it - for the sale date, the lot and title, the 1952 dating, the verso particulars against which the documentation photographs are tested, the estimate, and five times over for what it does not say, which is that it does not name Sun and Rain Works. Until now it was held as a live URL and nothing else. It has been fetched, and what it said on the date of fetching is now fixed: the page, its byte count and its SHA-256 are recorded in a manifest under data/evidence/, together with a transcription of the particulars the records depend on. The page itself is third-party commercial content and is not republished. Three things were confirmed at capture. The verso description matches the studio's photographs, including the frame in which the whole inscription is legible. The estimate of 7,000 to 10,000 US dollars matches the figure the auction house gave in its message of 2008-08-15, so that figure now has two independent sources. And the entry names no consignor, so the negative statement this collection repeats is true of the page as fetched. One detail is new: the catalogue attributes the 'Wells Fargo Bank' annotation to Adams himself. The gap is now closed, and how it closed is worth recording. When this passage was first written the Internet Archive held no capture of lot 16 in any form, while the catalogue around it was archived:  The same sale has been crawled from 2016 to 2025, ten of its lots have some capture and seven have a successful one, among them lots 27, 44, 61, 73 and 78; the locale-qualified form of these URLs is crawled too, lot 27 having been taken twice in that form. So the archive reaches this catalogue and has done for nine years. Lot 16 was a hole in covered ground rather than an unarchived site, and it was a small thing to fix. It was fixed on 2026-09-21, when the page was captured from a logged-in archive.org account at 02:28:20 UTC, together with a locale-qualified form of the same address a few minutes later which carries the same content digest. The snapshot has been fetched and read: it carries the lot number, the title, the American Trust Company stamp, the number 1-SF-46, the Wells Fargo annotation, the 1952 dating and the estimate of 7,000 to 10,000 US dollars, which is every particular the records here depend on. So the anchor no longer rests on a commercial auction house keeping a 2009 page online: a copy is held by a third party with no stake in this collection, and it can be cited beside the live address. One detail of the earlier attempt is kept rather than tidied away. An anonymous save was requested on 2026-09-20 and refused, because anonymous archiving now requires an account; that is why the page went unarchived while its neighbours did not, and why the capture had to be made by someone signed in. The publisher was never the obstacle - the site's robots.txt disallows only the lot PDF and carries no archiver exclusion. The studio's own fetch of 2026-09-20 is still worth having, for a different reason than before: it fixes what the page said a day before the archive's copy was taken, so the two can be compared. A note on the address, since two forms of it circulate. The page answers both at the bare lot path and with a locale=en query appended, and on the capture date the two served byte-identical content, the same 42,070 bytes under the same SHA-256. Either may be cited and the checksum recorded here covers both." .
_:r21b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r21b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
