_:r23b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: correspondence declaring Metaconceptual Art to a university art museum (2013)" .
_:r23b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r23b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r23b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r23b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r23b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r23b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r23b10 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r23b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-museum-correspondence-2013" .
_:r23b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r23b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r23b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: retained by the receiving institution and produced by it on request, published here in redacted form, with machine-generated headers that corroborate the date internally. The message carries five timestamps written by two unrelated mail systems, in monotonic order. Four are Yahoo message identifiers whose leading integers are server-written Unix timestamps: 1379027969, 1379028560, 1379028776 and 1379028931, decoding to 2013-09-12 at 23:19:29, 23:29:20, 23:32:56 and 23:35:31 UTC - 16:19 to 16:35 Pacific, the studio's local time, spanning sixteen minutes of one outbound thread. The fifth is the recipient Exchange server's own stamp on the reply, 'Thu, 12 Sep 2013 23:38:09 +0000', two and a half minutes after the last of them; the quoted 'Sent' line reads 4:36 PM, agreeing to within a minute. Yahoo generated those identifiers and the University of Oregon's server copied them verbatim into its References and In-Reply-To headers, so each system corroborates the other. The dating therefore does not rest on the studio's account: it would require consistent, ordered forgery across two independent providers." .
_:r23b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r23b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r23b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Publication and custody. This was private correspondence, not something its author published, so the message is not reproduced here in full. A redacted copy is published at data/evidence/museum-correspondence-2013/, withholding the replying staff member's name and both mailbox addresses while retaining the email domains, every header bearing on the date, the full text of the studio principal's declaration, and the substance of the museum's reply. The unredacted original is held by the studio on separate media, and its SHA-256 is published in that manifest so the held file can be shown to be unaltered without being disclosed; it can be released, in whole or in part, if the third party consents. Custody, corrected. An earlier version of this record stated that the sending mailbox had been closed and that the studio's saved file was therefore the only copy known to exist. That was wrong, and wrong in this project's favour. The file described in the manifest as the unredacted original is not a copy saved from the sending mailbox in 2013: it is the copy the receiving museum retrieved from its own archive and supplied in July 2026, and the two are the same file. Its headers settle the provenance. It carries no Received headers and none of the sending provider's X-headers, which a mailbox-saved copy would have; it carries four X-MS-Exchange-Organization headers, including the originating client and server addresses, which exist only inside the university's own mail organisation, and the originating client address falls inside UONET, 128.223.0.0/16, the University of Oregon's allocation in the ARIN registry. The message is therefore not studio-held evidence. The institution named in it retained it for close to thirteen years and produced it on request, and because the museum's reply quotes the studio principal's letter in full, the university's own archive carries the text of the declaration." .
_:r23b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r23b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r23b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the correspondence does and does not establish. It establishes that the declaration existed in writing on 2013-09-12 and was delivered to a named art institution, which is the earliest such delivery the studio has produced. It does not establish that the movement originated in 2007, and it is not itself evidence of anything before its own date; the 2007 origin remains asserted by the studio. The 'May 2009' dating of the auction in this letter is incorrect. Sotheby's own record gives sale N08533, Photographs, as 30 March 2009 in New York, 169 lots. The letter's month is therefore the artist's misrecollection, written four years after the event and before the catalogue record had been consulted. The 2008 consignment correspondence supplies part of the explanation: throughout that thread Sotheby's described the sale as its April 2009 sale, and the consignment was arranged against an April date. The sale actually took place on 30 March. A recollection of late spring rather than March is what an artist working from the plan of record would be left with, which makes the error the ordinary kind rather than a careless one. It remains an error; the letter says May, and no document says May. The error is confined to the month: the sale number, the lot number, the photographer, and the title given in the letter all match the catalogue entry. The same month appears in the artist's public Flickr profile, written in the same state of knowledge. Sun & Rain Works has elected to leave that profile unaltered, as an artifact of what was believed at the time rather than a statement to be brought into line with the record; the two texts are consequently left disagreeing with the catalogue and agreeing with each other. Neither is corrected here. An uncorrected error carried across two independent self-descriptions is itself evidence of the kind of account it is - remembered rather than constructed - and revising it after the fact would remove that. His statement that eight defining characteristics were already publicly findable in September 2013 is likewise his own, and the material he referred to has not been located." .
_:r23b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r23b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r23b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The museum's role is receipt, and nothing more. The reply acknowledged the message and said it would be forwarded to staff. It records no exhibition, acquisition, review, endorsement, or acceptance of the work or of the term, and none should be inferred from this record: the Jordan Schnitzer Museum of Art is named here because the identity of the receiving institution is what gives the delivery its evidential weight, not because it participated in the work. Following this corpus's convention, the individual member of staff who replied is identified by role only; their name and the correspondents' email addresses are withheld from this public record and retained in the studio's private copy. The saved thread preserves the text parts only: the letter refers to attached images, which are not present in the file held." .
_:r23b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r23b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r23b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A negative finding, recorded because it bears on a claim this collection makes elsewhere. On 2026-07-11 the studio asked the museum whether its email archive reached back to March 2009, saying that Sun & Rain Works had sent it a press release around the Sotheby's Photographs sale of 30 March 2009. On 2026-07-15 the museum's communications manager replied that the museum holds no email from the studio's then-current address dated 2009, and attached the 2013 thread instead. No institutional corroboration for a 2009 approach to this museum therefore exists. The finding should be read for exactly what it is: a search that returned nothing, not a demonstration that nothing was sent. A 2009 message could have been addressed to staff whose mail was not searched, or have fallen outside a retention window, or have predated the present holder's tenure. What can be said is that the museum's holdings do reach 2013, that they were searched on request, and that nothing from 2009 came back. The studio's account of a 2009 press release to this institution remains unsupported by the recipient's own records." .
_:r23b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r23b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r23b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the letter names, and what it does not. The letter is the only document in this collection in which the studio sets out its titled works to an institution. It names two: 'Two popular examples of Metaconceptual Art, which are ongoing through time and space as well, are titled \"Art Market Reform\" , and \"Art Tax\"', and glosses the first as promoting accountability, transparency and regulation in the art market. It names its business, 'Sun and Rain Works', and gives the writer's role there as 'Chief Executive Artist'. It reports that a proposal for both works went to the White House two days earlier, which this collection has not verified and does not assert. And it mentions the 2009 sale, in the same letter, without giving it a title, calling it a performance act of Metaconceptual Art. The naming is the earliest dated use of 'Art Tax' held here and the earliest delivery of either title to an institution; 'Art Market Reform' is used earlier on Facebook, publicly on 2012-08-14 and as a named work of Metaconceptual Art in a private message of 2013-05-29, both set out in the record of the 2012-2013 Facebook and USA Projects evidence, and the absence of a title for the auction is recorded in the concept record as evidence rather than as silence." .
_:r23b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r23b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r23b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r23b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r23b10 .
_:r23b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r23b9 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the 2013 museum-correspondence evidence record" .
_:r24b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> .
_:r24b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P75i_is_possessed_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r24b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E30_Right> .
_:r24b0 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "CC BY 4.0" .
_:r24b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: screenshots of the studio's outreach, 12-13 September 2013" .
_:r24b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r24b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r24b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r24b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Publication and redaction. The four screenshots are published here as redacted copies: /images/linkedin-art-market-reform-2013.png, /images/artspace-page-2013.png, /images/sothebys-locations-2013.png and /images/arts-commission-reply-2013.png. The redactions cover the studio's own mailbox names and addresses in the browser's tab and title bars; in the LinkedIn screenshot, the names and photographs of other group members in the 'Top Influencers' and 'Latest Activity' panels; and in the reply, the staff member's name, both staff mailbox addresses, and the direct office telephone number. Nothing else was altered. The published files are re-encoded copies, so their checksums differ from those of the originals given above. The unredacted originals are held by the studio on separate media." .
_:r24b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r24b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r24b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What these screenshots do not show. They are not the messages themselves: no message file with headers is held for the Arts Commission exchange, the sending mailbox is no longer available to the studio, and no copy has been requested from the Commission. They date nothing before September 2013 and are not evidence of the 2007 origin, of the 2009 sale as an artwork, or of any White House submission. The LinkedIn post and the message to the Commission are the artist's own declarations, of the same kind as the museum letter; the one outside voice in the set is a courteous reply that takes no position on the work." .
_:r24b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r24b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r24b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r24b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r24b13 .
_:r24b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r24b12 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the 2013 outreach-screenshots evidence record" .
_:r24b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r24b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r24b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r24b13 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r24b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-outreach-screenshots-2013" .
_:r24b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r24b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r24b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: held by the studio as screenshots, recovered from a USB flash drive by file carving, and each accompanied on that drive by a small metadata file of its own. Each image is a 1,280 by 800 pixel PNG carrying nothing inside it but an embedded colour profile and a pixel-density field: no capture date, no software tag, and no XMP. The embedded profile is the Mac's own display profile, which macOS generated on 2013-01-06 at 15:51:44, and it is identical in all four files; that is a floor under all four, if the profile belongs to the machine that took them, and evidence that one machine took all four. The held originals are f0040080.png (the LinkedIn screenshot), 759,660 bytes, SHA-256 7d2a0d0b61a77b3aea8a77761249fce228d9f0bc9e65a043fc642045a85fd725; f0044816.png (the Artspace page), 528,496 bytes, SHA-256 b25562b51655a7e73a66c7d76819eb47e30db78d9547218aab2423045b449441; f0046672.png (the Sotheby's page), 436,785 bytes, SHA-256 6e63fa6e87745923f07ffd45961609bc93c4d8a7f562254b0f0062959325f991; and f0047824.png (the Arts Commission reply), 436,804 bytes, SHA-256 48e65d283878e9e09ecc7ce40516b76750a93a0d977149b1b01fa66eae5facac. The names are the carving tool's, which numbers each recovered file by its position on the drive. The drive's own directory, read directly from a sector image of it, still records the names the Mac gave the four files when it took them: 'Screen shot 2013-09-12 at 3.49.13 PM.png', 'Screen shot 2013-09-13 at 12.43.10 PM.png', 'Screen shot 2013-09-13 at 2.46.07 PM.png' and 'Screen shot 2013-09-13 at 3.09.14 PM.png', each entry giving the same size in bytes as its image. A Mac names a screenshot for the moment it is taken, so those names carry the capture times directly." .
_:r24b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r24b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r24b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The companion metadata files. When a Mac writes a file to a drive formatted for Windows, it stores the file's extended attributes in a separate hidden companion file in the AppleDouble format. The same carving recovered 58 such companions from the drive. They carry no file names, but on this drive each sits exactly one 32-kilobyte cluster before the file it describes, and the companion in that position before each of the four screenshots was decoded. All four record the macOS attribute marking the file as a screen capture of the whole display, so the images are what they appear to be: screenshots taken by the Mac's own screenshot function, not images assembled some other way. All four also carry a quarantine attribute naming Preview, Apple's image viewer, as the application responsible, with timestamps of 2014-09-18 at 21:59:34 UTC (Artspace), 22:00:11 (Sotheby's), 22:01:23 (the Arts Commission reply) and 22:02:30 (LinkedIn): the four files were written out again through Preview within three minutes on that day, a year after they were taken. That explains why the images no longer carry the metadata a Mac writes into a screenshot when it first saves one, and it places all four in existence by 18 September 2014. Those timestamps are the Mac's clock, so they are the studio's own account, not a third party's. The pairing of companion to image rests on their positions on the drive, and is stated as that. The four companion files are held as f0044752.apple, f0046608.apple, f0047760.apple and f0040016.apple, 32,768 bytes each as carved, with SHA-256 8425c85dc226e963bc2dacfed3bf2cec55c76bcb00ce62778e21cd4aa9be43bb, 42b91602ee4e2aeba4fb7b41faf52e34201ba2032f00359b72c5f7b3a95c9ad5, 60c10665ca747f1ce5b6f93ffddedf55bd0065a93302d1c9fc587a3716771c08 and 514fdbd415adb7893532cef98ff74525bff477ebb3624b2896891015b4d2a8d7 respectively." .
_:r24b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r24b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r24b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "How the dates are fixed, and how firmly. Only one date in the set is printed in full: the mail client's header on the Arts Commission reply, Fri, Sep 13, 2013, 2:58 PM. The screenshot of it shows the Mac's menu-bar clock at Fri 3:09 PM, eleven minutes later, and the dock's calendar icon at 13. The Artspace screenshot shows Fri 12:43 PM with the calendar at 13 and the webmail tab at 3,323 unread, which falls between the 3,302 shown on the Thursday and the counts later on the Friday afternoon, so it was taken about two hours before the others that day. The Sotheby's screenshot shows Fri 2:46 PM with the calendar at 13, the same four other tabs open in the same order, and the webmail tab's unread count one lower, 3,329 against 3,330, so it was taken twenty-three minutes before the reply screenshot on the same afternoon. The LinkedIn screenshot shows Thu 3:49 PM with the calendar at 12, the same browser and menu-bar items, and the LinkedIn post is still open as a tab in both Friday screenshots; its date, Thursday 12 September 2013, was first inferred from that sequence and is now confirmed by the name the Mac gave the file, 'Screen shot 2013-09-12 at 3.49.13 PM'. The browser's appearance is Firefox before its redesign of April 2014, which agrees. On those grounds the set is dated 12-13 September 2013, and the dates on screen and in the file names agree. The times are the Mac's local clock, and that clock was on Pacific Daylight Time: the Spotlight index on the same drive, which records times in UTC, gives the LinkedIn screenshot as 22:49:12 UTC, seven hours ahead of the 3:49 PM in its name. The LinkedIn post therefore went up about half an hour before the first message to the museum, whose headers place it at 16:19 Pacific the same day." .
_:r24b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r24b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r24b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A fifth screenshot, now lost. The drive's directory and its Spotlight index both record a fifth screenshot from the same afternoon, 'Screen shot 2013-09-13 at 3.20.44 PM.png', 508,109 bytes, which the index gives as 22:20:44 UTC, eleven minutes after the screenshot of the Arts Commission reply. Its contents have since been overwritten on the drive, no copy is held, and what it showed is unknown. It is recorded so that the set is known to have had five members, not four." .
_:r24b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r24b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r24b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The LinkedIn post, in its own words. The post opens: 'My name is Joseph M. Chirum, and i am writing to begin, encourage, and promote an open discussion regarding \"Art Market Reform\" and \"Art Tax\".' It then says that in May 2009 he auctioned lot 16 of Sotheby's New York photograph auction, Ansel Adams's 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks', 'as a performance act of Metaconceptual Art'; that Metaconceptual Art has eight defining characteristics findable on Google or YouTube; that he had submitted a proposal for 'Art Market Reform' and 'Art Tax' to the White House two days earlier and was awaiting feedback; and that when they spoke on the telephone he was asked to find local parties and support for the proposal, which is the purpose of the discussion. The wording follows the museum letter of the same afternoon closely, including the May dating of the sale, which is wrong: Sotheby's sale N08533 took place on 30 March 2009. The error is left as written here, as it is in the museum record. The telephone call and the White House submission are the studio's statements; this collection has not verified either and does not assert them. The studio's LinkedIn profile has no archived copy and can no longer be reached, so this screenshot is the only copy of the post the studio holds." .
_:r24b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r24b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r24b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The Arts Commission reply. The reply is from the Oregon Arts Commission's Assistant Director, with one colleague copied, and answers a message from the studio headed 'Metaconceptual Art project discussion follow up'; a second tab in the same screenshot shows an automatic reply to a message whose subject also begins 'Met'. The studio's own message is not shown. What the reply establishes is narrow: that on 13 September 2013 a state arts agency received the studio's approach about the Metaconceptual Art project, that the studio had told it of a proposal to the White House, and that the agency answered with a referral to its grants programme. It records no endorsement, partnership, funding, or assessment of the work, and none should be inferred. The agency is named because the identity of the recipient is what gives the exchange its weight; the individuals are identified by role only, following this collection's convention." .
_:r24b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r24b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r24b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The Artspace and Sotheby's pages. These two screenshots show only that the studio had Artspace's home page open at midday on 13 September 2013, showing the gallery's call for artists for its street-facing window project and its list of sponsors, and the Sotheby's locations page open that afternoon, each beside a Wikipedia list of contemporary art museums. They are not evidence of any contact with Artspace or with Sotheby's in 2013, and none is claimed. Artspace is named only because its page is what the screenshot shows; it is not a party to this collection." .
_:r24b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r24b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r25b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> .
_:r25b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P75i_is_possessed_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r25b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E30_Right> .
_:r25b0 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "CC BY 4.0" .
_:r25b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: public postings of Metaconceptual Art (2011-2012)" .
_:r25b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r25b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r25b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r25b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "An outstanding lead, recorded so it is not lost. The studio holds three chat logs downloaded from meetings of that period, sent to a correspondent on 2012-11-12 as attachments named for their dates: two of 2011-11-10 and 2011-11-19, and a third of 2011-11-19. Those dates fall eighteen and nine days before the posting of 2011-11-28, which is the earliest located public use of the term; an earlier, private use, of 2009-12-19, is set out in the record of the 2009 Facebook evidence. The logs have not been examined. If the term, the eight characteristics, or the concept in recognisable form appears in them, the earliest evidence in this collection moves earlier, and it moves into a different and better kind of source: conversation with other people, rather than the studio's own solo posting. If it does not appear, that is a negative worth recording with the same care." .
_:r25b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r25b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r25b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r25b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r25b12 .
_:r25b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r25b11 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the 2011-2012 public-posting evidence record" .
_:r25b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r25b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r25b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r25b12 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r25b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-pastebin-2011-2012" .
_:r25b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r25b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r25b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: publicly verifiable on a third-party platform, and the earliest such evidence in this collection. The postings are readable by anyone at https://pastebin.com/AmQ3P87v and https://pastebin.com/18ndp2CF ; both are set never to expire. Their dates are assigned by the platform at the moment of posting and are carried in the page markup as 'Thursday 22nd of November 2012 11:19:57 PM CDT' and 'Monday 28th of November 2011 07:51:51 AM CDT'. Unlike a profile biography, the body of a paste is fixed once posted. The platform also records public circulation: 950 views for the 2012 posting and 433 for the 2011 one at the time of compilation. Nothing here is studio-held, so verification requires no access to the private archive." .
_:r25b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r25b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r25b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Relation to the published Eight Sentences. The 2012 posting is the earliest located instance of the sentence sequence and predates its publication on this site by fourteen years. That there were eight characteristics is attested twenty-three days earlier, in a private Facebook message of 2012-10-30 which refers to them without listing them, set out in the record of the 2012-2013 Facebook and USA Projects evidence. Its wording is the wording published here, allowing for three trivial variances: 'commodification of Art, and its relationship' has since lost its comma, 'the Art World , and Art History' has lost a space, and 'Transcendance' has been corrected to 'Transcendence'. The persistence of a misspelling across the interval, and its later silent normalisation, are recorded because they are the sort of detail by which a text's descent is traced. The sequence's stated debt to Sol LeWitt's 'Sentences on Conceptual Art' is not present in the 2012 posting and appears to be a later framing." .
_:r25b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r25b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r25b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A second, independent timestamp for the 2011 posting. The studio's Facebook account data, compiled by Facebook on request on 2026-09-24, records that at 2011-11-28 15:44:21 UTC the account 'liked' a link titled 'The Art World is Flat - Pastebin.com', pointing to http://pastebin.com/18ndp2CF, the posting described above. That is within three hours of the posting time Pastebin displays, 07:51:51 AM on the same day. The like is Facebook's record, made on its own servers, so the posting's date no longer rests on Pastebin's alone, and it shows the posting being marked from the studio's own account on the day it was made. The entry is in the export's reactions file, 4,891,632 bytes, SHA-256 b4199322474511c9db9892c812ff17ec641702b8aee7826e6944bdbf15c57924, within the archive facebook-joechirum-2026-09-24-TZ87gGfq.zip, SHA-256 c978c3bd338a6a9a934d3aa05a81d2c298c49200bf7e4edc98b8329c02103097." .
_:r25b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r25b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r25b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Limits. Both postings are the artist's own; the platform dates when he said something publicly, not whether what he said was true. They therefore evidence the public existence of the term and its definition at those dates, and nothing about the 2009 auction, which neither posting mentions. The 2011 posting describes its poem as written 'a few years ago', which would place composition somewhere in the late 2000s; that is the artist's own statement, made in 2011 rather than in retrospect from the present, and it narrows but does not close the gap to the asserted 2007 origin. The origin date itself remains unevidenced. Both postings link to a Sun & Rain Works page on Facebook. That page has since been deleted by the studio, and the Internet Archive holds no capture of it, so it cannot be examined and any earlier material it carried is lost. Its former existence is not in doubt: the 2012 posting cites its address and presents the eight characteristics as the page's content, which places the definition on that page on or before 2012-11-22. The postings recorded here are consequently the surviving witness to a source that no longer exists, and they are load-bearing for that reason." .
_:r25b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r25b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r25b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Preservation note. Of the sources that once carried this material, several no longer exist: the Facebook page has been deleted, the YouTube videos cited in the 2013 correspondence are gone, and the mailbox from which that correspondence was sent has been closed. What survives does so on a third-party platform the studio does not control, in two postings set never to expire. Their continued availability is not guaranteed by anyone. The studio has therefore taken a local copy of both postings - the paste bodies and the rendered pages, the latter retained because they carry the platform's own timestamp string that the raw endpoint omits - published alongside this record with checksums at data/evidence/pastebin-2011-2012/. Those files are a preservation copy and not the dated source: a local file proves what was retrieved and when it was retrieved, and nothing more. The dating rests on Pastebin's records, which assign a posting timestamp the poster cannot alter, and should be verified against the live URLs for as long as they resolve." .
_:r25b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r25b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r25b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Context for November 2011, independently dated. The studio states that in the autumn of 2011 it set up and ran two wiki sites for the Occupy movement, at wikioccupy.org and wikioccupy.com. Part of that is verifiable without reference to the studio: the Internet Archive first reached wikioccupy.org on 2011-10-24, captured pages of the running wiki on 2011-10-25, and captured its main page, 'Wiki Occupy Home', on 2011-10-31, which gives as its contact an address at the site's own domain under the name 'Art'. No capture of wikioccupy.com exists, and both domains have since lapsed and are now unregistered. An earlier version of this record gave a capture of 2011-12-03 as the earliest and read it as the live site; that capture returns only the hosting provider's default page, and the earlier captures were found when every address under the domain was searched. The site and the chat logs of November 2011 in which the studio principal described it as art are set out in the record of www.wikioccupy.org." .
_:r25b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r25b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r25b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What that context does and does not do. It does not evidence Metaconceptual Art. Running a wiki for a protest movement is not making art, and nothing on the archived site has been shown to concern the studio's work. Its relevance is to reading, not to dating: the posting of 'The Art World is Flat' on 2011-11-28 was made in the same weeks that its author was, on independent evidence, operating web infrastructure for a movement whose subject was finance and inequality. A poem about the flatness of the art world, published in honour of Cyber Monday by someone then running an Occupy wiki, is more legible for knowing that. Two smaller points also follow. The studio's account of the wiki is a claim it did not control the checking of, and it checks out, which is a modest reason to take its other accounts of that period seriously. And a person who registered and ran domains for projects in 2011 is the kind of person who might have done so for www.wtfisart.com in 2009, which makes that claim more plausible in kind without dating it by a single day." .
_:r25b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r25b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r26b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: a painted postcard inscribed 'For the Love of Art' and signed 2008 (photographed 2011)" .
_:r26b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r26b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r26b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r26b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-postcard-work-2008" .
_:r26b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r26b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r26b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: held by the studio; a derived copy, not the camera's original file. The file is held as ArtFlowers(AcrylicandInkonPostcard).JPEG, 338,239 bytes, SHA-256 e58a9d0872a28f05b3758bede1302af64051f8facb4bb772a57c95347296a7ac. Its Exif names the camera as an LG Electronics LG-VX8575 with firmware VX857V06, the same phone and firmware as the photograph of 2011-11-08 described in the inscription-photograph record, and gives DateTimeOriginal and DateTimeDigitized as 2011:11:16 07:29:20. Three things show that the held file is not what the camera wrote. Its dimensions are 1,600 by 1,217 pixels, where that phone writes 2,048 by 1,536, and the proportions are not the camera's, so the image has been cropped; the Exif dimension fields were rewritten to match, which the cropping software did; and the camera's embedded thumbnail is gone. The file name is descriptive rather than the phone's own date-built name, so it was also renamed. The capture fields survived that processing, but nothing in the file corroborates them, as the photograph of 2011-11-08 is corroborated by its own file name and thumbnail. The date is therefore the file's account, at one remove. The photograph is published here at /images/for-the-love-of-art-postcard-2008.jpg, a byte-identical copy of the held file, renamed for the web and not otherwise altered, so the checksum describes the published image as well. No person, face, address or third party's private matter is visible in it." .
_:r26b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r26b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r26b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What is written on the work, and what is printed under it. The handwritten text is legible in full: 'For the Love of Art, these tulips are beautiful! Almost as good as in real life', in quotation marks, then 'J.M.C.' and '08' at the right, then 'P.S. \"Double the Fun\"'. The signature on the painted side reads 'Joe Chirum' above '08'. The postcard's own printed caption is only partly legible at this resolution: the artist's name Cezanne with dates, a title naming tulips, a canvas measurement, a named memorial collection, and a copyright line for an art museum in Chicago; a printed line along the inner edge reads 'Great Impressionist Painting Postcards' with that museum's copyright. The postcard series' year of publication is not legible, and no attempt is made here to supply the missing words from a catalogue. The reproduction is a third party's published postcard, and the museum's copyright line appears on it. The painting reproduced is by an artist who died in 1906 and is itself long out of copyright; the postcard appears in this photograph as the support of the studio's own work, which is painted and written over it. The museum's notice is recorded here rather than dismissed, and the photograph is published on that footing." .
_:r26b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r26b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r26b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The date of the work is the work's own statement. '08' is written twice on the object, once beside the initials on the inscription and once under the signature. That is the artist's own dating, made on the work, and it is not independent of the artist. No document in this collection dates the postcard's making, and the photograph that records it was taken three years later. So the position is: the work says 2008, and the earliest date any record here can fix for it is 2011-11-16. If the postcard series can be identified and its publication year read, that would give a floor below which the work cannot have been made, and that is worth doing." .
_:r26b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r26b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r26b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the inscription does and does not join. 'For the Love of Art' is a phrase about art written on a work, as 'W.T.F. is Art ?' is on the sheet photographed eight days earlier and 'WTF is Art ?!' is on the Art Notes board. The three are not the same phrase and this record does not treat them as one: what they share is the practice of writing a line about art onto the work itself, which is visible across the studio's objects from at least 2011 by the files' own dates. The postcard adds one thing the others do not. It is made on a museum's reproduction of a famous painting, painted over and then addressed back to art, which is the same gesture the collection describes elsewhere as working on the institution's own material. Whether the studio intended that reading in 2008 is the studio's account and is not recorded in any document of the time." .
_:r26b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r26b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r26b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What this record does not show. It does not date the making of the work; it fixes only that the work existed, framed, by 2011-11-16, on the file's own account. It does not show that anyone outside the studio ever saw the work, and no public posting of it has been found at the date of this record. It says nothing about the domain wtfisart.com, nothing about the 2009 auction designation, and nothing about the asserted 2007 origin of Metaconceptual Art. Whether the physical work survives is not established here." .
_:r26b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r26b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r26b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r26b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r26b8 .
_:r26b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r26b7 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the painted postcard record" .
_:r26b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r26b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r26b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r26b8 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r27b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> .
_:r27b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P75i_is_possessed_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r27b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E30_Right> .
_:r27b0 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "CC BY 4.0" .
_:r27b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: the press release 'Metaconceptual Art creates Hedge Fund Art and paints Art Market Reform' (May 2014)" .
_:r27b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r27b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r27b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r27b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-press-release-2014" .
_:r27b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r27b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r27b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: the sending dated by Facebook, the document held by the studio. The two messages are in the account data Facebook compiled and delivered on request on 2026-09-24, held by the studio as facebook-joechirum-2026-09-24-TZ87gGfq.zip, SHA-256 c978c3bd338a6a9a934d3aa05a81d2c298c49200bf7e4edc98b8329c02103097; each is the only message in its thread, and the thread files, of 2,205 and 3,091 bytes, have SHA-256 0661610d6bfd07db3c600ce8c1633cef5c0fd8ed29d23419f8446d71889315ae and 6048ca3620a329097828fdb86cc4b7f789f9aa7747c050cc88f423af3d847605. The Word file, ArtPressRelease.docx, 108,973 bytes, SHA-256 71f6be05fa04f9f5e31f798df2ca459fdb4f3f8ed51dfd8ce0c0f3fcfb01784c, was recovered from the studio's USB flash drive, byte-identical, by two separate recovery tools. Its internal properties give Microsoft Word for Mac 14 as the application, the Mac account 'Meta Art' as author and last editor, creation at 2014-05-18T20:16:00Z and last save at 2014-05-20T13:01:00Z. Those two times are the Mac's clock and are the file's own account, not a third party's; the creation time falls between the two messages, which is consistent with them." .
_:r27b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r27b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r27b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Three versions, in order. The first message, at 13:27 UTC, is a personal note to its recipient carrying the release's body: the goal, the paradigm sentence, the eight characteristics and the Saatchi link. The second, at 22:42 UTC, is the release as a formatted document headed 'For Immediate Press Release', dated 5-18-2014, with the headline and the quotation. The Word file, dated 20 May 2014 in its text, adds a second heading, 'For: International Interdisciplinary Collaborative Research', and closes with 'For the Love of God, We are the Art…' and a link to a video. The first message spells the sixth characteristic 'Transcendance', as the public posting of 2012-11-22 does; the second message and the Word file spell it 'Transcendence', as the text published here does, so the correction can be placed on 18 May 2014." .
_:r27b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r27b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r27b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A variant in the eight characteristics. In all three versions of 2014 the seventh and eighth characteristics read 'The blurring of the boundaries between Art World insiders and Outsiders' and 'The blurring of the boundaries between Art and Life'. The public posting of 2012-11-22 and the Eight Sentences published here read 'The annihilating of the boundaries' in both. The 2014 release therefore circulated a softer wording of those two characteristics than the text posted in 2012 and published now. The difference is recorded as found; this record does not say which wording the studio regards as authoritative." .
_:r27b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r27b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r27b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the release does and does not show. It shows the studio presenting Metaconceptual Art to people in the art world as a movement, with a definition and named works, on 2014-05-18, in the same week it uploaded photographs of the works to Flickr and listed them on Saatchi Art. It is the studio's own statement throughout: the claims that it is 'an entire Art movement' and 'the Art historical paradigm, which comes after Conceptual Art' are its claims, and no outlet is known to have published the release or to have answered it. It says nothing about the 2009 sale and does not evidence the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere." .
_:r27b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r27b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r27b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Privacy. The two recipients are not named, and the note to the first recipient is described rather than quoted. The telephone number the release gives as its contact is withheld. Only the release's own wording is reproduced." .
_:r27b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r27b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r27b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r27b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r27b9 .
_:r27b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r27b8 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the 2014 press-release evidence record" .
_:r27b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r27b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r27b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r27b9 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r28b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: Sun and Rain Works in a market's membership records (2005, 2006, 2008)" .
_:r28b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r28b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r28b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r28b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-saturday-market-membership" .
_:r28b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r28b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r28b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: an institution speaking from its own records, and the earliest such statement in this collection. Its weight comes from three things. The market is not the studio and has no stake in the studio's claims. The years it gives are drawn from membership records it keeps for its own purposes, not from anything the studio supplied to it. And the statement is re-checkable: the market still exists, still holds the records, and can be asked again by anyone. Against that, one limit must be stated. The reply is held only as a screenshot of a mail client; the message itself has not been examined, no headers or checksum are published, and the exchange is therefore reported rather than verified in the way the 2013 museum correspondence has been. Obtaining the message file, or a written statement on the market's letterhead, would close that gap." .
_:r28b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r28b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r28b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What it establishes, and what it does not. It establishes that a business trading as Sun and Rain Works existed and was selling work in Eugene, Oregon in 2005, six years before the earliest public use of the term Metaconceptual Art and three years before the studio's first documented approach to an art institution about the Adams photograph. It removes a question that would otherwise sit under every earlier claim in this collection, which is whether the studio existed as a working art business at the dates it describes. It does not touch Metaconceptual Art at all. A market's membership roll records who paid to sell at its stalls; it says nothing about what anyone thought, wrote, or made, and no part of it evidences the 2007 origin this collection asserts elsewhere. The market's records also say nothing about who held the membership beyond the business name. A photograph held by the studio agrees with the market's entry for 2006, on the studio's own dating. It is a colour print, held as a scan of 1,642 by 1,203 pixels, 487,119 bytes, SHA-256 8fb9d61a900bdf17fc41a43cd6357eebfbef2c2c781a703957fd13a6ea1c024b, made on 2025-02-19 according to its file name. It shows the studio principal seated in an outdoor market stall under a blue tarpaulin, hung with tie-dyed shirts and garments, and it carries a date imprinted by the camera, '06 9 9, which reads as 9 September 2006, a Saturday. The date is the camera's own setting and is not independent of the photographer, and nothing in the frame names the market or the business. So the photograph shows the studio principal selling tie-dyed work at a market stall on a date the camera gives as a Saturday in 2006, which is consistent with the market's record for that year; it adds no date that the market's statement does not already give. It is not published, because a second person appears, in part, at the edge of the frame." .
_:r28b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r28b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r28b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A coincidence, recorded and not interpreted. The years given are 2005, 2006 and 2008. The one year missing from that run is 2007, which is the year this collection asserts as the origin of Metaconceptual Art. Nothing is drawn from this. Market memberships lapse for ordinary reasons - a season away, a change of circumstances, a year not trading - and no document connects the gap to the assertion. It is noted here because a reader comparing the two dates will notice it, and because a record that omitted it would be less honest than one that does not." .
_:r28b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r28b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r28b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Redaction. Following this collection's convention, the market's general manager, who answered the inquiry in an official capacity, is identified by role only; their name, direct mailbox and telephone number are withheld pending consent and retained in the studio's private copy. The organisation itself is named, because the identity of the attesting body is what gives the statement its weight. No endorsement by the Eugene Saturday Market of this project, of Metaconceptual Art, or of any claim in this collection is implied or should be inferred: the market answered a factual question about its own membership records and nothing more." .
_:r28b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r28b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r28b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r28b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r28b7 .
_:r28b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r28b6 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the market membership record" .
_:r28b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r28b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r28b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r28b7 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r29b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: the approach to a museum photography department (11 August 2008)" .
_:r29b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r29b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r29b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r29b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-sfmoma-approach-2008" .
_:r29b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r29b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r29b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The closing 'For the Love of Art'. The studio's account, given in 2026, is that the sign-off was a deliberate adaptation of the title of Damien Hirst's For the Love of God (2007), with 'Art' in place of 'God', and not a chance valediction. This letter does not name Hirst, and 'for the love of' is an ordinary idiom, so the letter alone cannot confirm the reference; the studio did, however, use the unaltered title in its own later material, the notes panel and the wtfisart.com captures of 2014 and 2016. The evidence for and against the account is weighed in the record of the approach to Sotheby's, which closes with the same words three days later. The account of intent is reported as the studio's, not as a fact this letter establishes." .
_:r29b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Two errors in the letter, recorded because they are evidence of the kind of document it is. The writer dates the photograph to 'what appears to be 1946'. The Sotheby's catalogue published seven months later gives 1952, printed probably in the 1950s. An earlier version of this record cited a shortcut file in the studio's archive, whose name carries the year 1946, as contemporaneous corroboration of that belief. That was wrong and is withdrawn: the shortcut was created on 2026-08-20 and points to a file on the present machine, so it records a recent naming choice and evidences nothing about 2008. The 1946 belief is attested by this letter alone, which is sufficient for the purpose - the letter is contemporaneous and states the belief in the writer's own words - and was later corrected by the auction house's cataloguing. Where the year came from is not recorded anywhere, and an explanation is now available which this record offers as a reading rather than as a fact. The verso of the print carries, in the photographer's printed credit block, the line 'NEGATIVE No. 1-SF-46'. That number is independently attested: the public catalogue entry for lot 16 of sale N08533 records the print as numbered 1-SF-46, and a photograph in which the whole line is legible is published with the documentation-photographs record. A writer examining the verso for information about a print he had just acquired, and finding a number ending in forty-six, might reasonably take it for a year; the letter's own hedge, 'from what appears to be 1946', is the phrasing of someone reading something off an object rather than recalling a fact he was told. Against that, nothing in the letter mentions the negative number, the inference is this collection's and not the writer's, and a person who misread a catalogue number as a date might equally have got the year from somewhere else entirely. The error is recorded here as explicable, not as explained. Second, the letter reports contact with a named person at 'the Ansel Adams Musem in SanFrancisco'. No institution of that name was operating in San Francisco in 2008: the Ansel Adams Center for Photography, run by the Friends of Photography, closed permanently in 2001 and the organisation dissolved. This is no longer an open question. The body the writer meant has since been identified: two messages of 2008-08-08, two days before this letter, were sent to a named representative at anseladams.com, which is the organisation bearing the photographer's name and is not a San Francisco museum. The contact this letter reports is therefore real and documented, and the error is confined to how the writer described the organisation. Neither error is material to anything this collection claims, and both are the ordinary imprecision of a person writing in real time rather than the tidiness of a document assembled afterwards." .
_:r29b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Redaction and scope. The individual named in the letter as a contact at an Adams institution is not named here; that name is withheld pending consent, following this collection's convention, and is retained in the studio's private copy, as are the writer's then-current mailbox and telephone number. The recipient institution is named, because the identity of the body approached is what gives the approach its meaning. No interest, response, endorsement, or acquisition by the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art is claimed or should be inferred: this record evidences an unanswered letter. It establishes nothing about the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection, and nothing about the auction, which had not been proposed when it was written." .
_:r29b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The artist's account of the word, recorded as his account and given in 2026. Sun & Rain Works states that the work was made under the influence of the Happenings of the 1960s, and that the word 'happening' in this letter was used in that sense deliberately. The statement differs from the studio's account of the concealment, recorded in the correspondence record, in one respect that matters: that account explained an absence, why nothing was said to the auction house, and could not be checked against anything. This one makes a claim about a specific word in a specific surviving document, and can therefore be set beside it. Two features of the letter are consistent with the account. The recipient is the photography department of a museum of modern art, which is the institutional home of that tradition rather than an incidental choice of correspondent. And the phrasing is marked: a writer meaning only an occurrence would more naturally have offered the museum the discovery or the photograph, whereas this sentence offers 'this happening' as the thing that might interest them, which is the situation rather than the object." .
_:r29b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Why that account cannot settle the question it addresses, stated plainly because it is the central difficulty in this collection. What is at issue is whether a framing was present in 2008 or arrived later. An artist's testimony given in 2026 about what he meant in 2008 is not independent of that question: it is the thing being asked about. Memory reconstructs, and it does so sincerely. A person who came to understand an ambiguous choice as deliberate some years after making it will report, honestly and without any intention to mislead, that it was deliberate at the time. Nothing in this record suggests that is what happened here; the point is that this record cannot distinguish it from the alternative, and neither can the testimony, because the two produce identical statements. The account is therefore recorded, weighed as consistent with the document, and treated as carrying little evidential weight of its own. Only something written before the framing was in question could carry more." .
_:r29b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b14 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A relevant negative from the working archive. The studio's source folder for www.wtfisart.com, ninety-eight files assembled between August 2008 and March 2009 and searched exhaustively in 2026, contains no occurrence of the word happening, none of performance, and none of conceptual art. Its content is art-market press, dealer directories and art-historical reference plates. If the Happenings of the 1960s were the operative influence in that period, the folder carries no trace of that particular lineage. One qualification belongs here, and it cuts the other way. The folder is not empty of the studio's stated concerns: 'IsItArt.doc' compiles, among its twenty-four press reports, an article quoting Andy Warhol's dictum that good business is the best art - the central idea of the volume the studio had composed into its documentation photographs five months earlier. So a conceptual lineage is present in the contemporaneous papers; it is Warhol and art-as-commerce rather than Kaprow and the Happening. This should not be pressed too far in either direction: the folder was assembled for a website about the art market, and a person does not research what he already knows. But an influence claimed for this period leaves no mark in the only substantial body of the studio's own contemporaneous papers that survives, and a record that omitted that would be selecting its evidence." .
_:r29b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b15 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b16 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status, restated after the messages were produced as files. This letter and its sibling of 2008-08-14 are now held as message files exported from the studio's own mailbox, where previously both were held only as screenshots. Both retain original Hotmail headers: a provider-stamped X-Originating-IP, a Date carrying a real local offset of minus seven hours, and no Message-ID or Exchange organisation headers, so neither has passed through a later platform. Every phrase this record quotes has been checked against them and agrees word for word, including the description of the writer as an artist and as the sole proprietor of a fledgeling Art business, and the sentence about a happening. The letter also names, in full, the person at the Adams organisation whom this record previously described only as a named individual; that name is withheld here under this collection's convention, and the messages sent to them two days earlier are the subject of a separate record. Two limits stand: neither file carries a Received chain, which is ordinary for Sent Items copies, and no reply from the museum is held or evidenced." .
_:r29b16 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b16 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b17 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r29b17 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r29b18 .
_:r29b17 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r29b17 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the museum approach record" .
_:r29b18 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r29b18 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r29b18 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r29b18 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r29b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: held as a screenshot of a mail client, and therefore reported rather than verified. No message file has been examined, no headers or Message-ID inspected, and no checksum is published. The screenshot shows a quoted header block giving sender, recipient, a send time of Monday, August 11, 2008 at 4:01 PM, and the subject. The recipient is an institutional mailbox at a museum which may hold its own copy. The studio put a records request to the museum on 2026-09-01 asking whether its holdings reach this message; no reply had been received when this record was written. If the museum produces a copy, this record moves from reported to institutionally retained, as the 2013 correspondence did, and should be revised accordingly. If the museum searches and finds nothing, that negative finding should be recorded here with its scope stated, as the 2009 negative finding is recorded in the museum correspondence record. No reply from the museum is held, and nothing in this collection shows that the museum answered, acknowledged, or acted on the message. What is evidenced is that the approach was made, not that it was received by any particular person or had any effect." .
_:r29b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A second view of the same message, added 2026-09-10. A further screenshot shows the message itself, open in the sent mail of the same mailbox rather than quoted inside a forward: the same sender, recipient, subject, body and closing, the attachment 'San Francisco.zip' at 3 MB, and a banner recording that the studio forwarded the message on 2024-09-03. Two things follow. First, the original is still held in a live mailbox, so it can be exported as a message file with its full headers and Message-ID, which is the step that would move this record from reported to verified. Second, this view displays the send time as 3:01 PM, an hour earlier than the 4:01 PM of the quoted header block. The raw Date line of the sibling message described below, 'Mon, 11 Aug 2008 16:01:02 -0700', is 23:01 UTC, which a client displays as 4:01 PM at UTC minus seven and as 3:01 PM at UTC minus eight. The difference is therefore the client's display setting, not a second send time: this view renders times at UTC minus eight, although the date falls in Pacific daylight time. A display setting describes the viewer, not the sender, so it says nothing about where the message was written. The calibration is useful beyond this record, because the consignment agreement record has a timestamp that turns on the same client's offset. The 2024 forward is not otherwise documented in this collection." .
_:r29b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "It moves two dates. Until this message was produced, the earliest dated instance of the name Sun and Rain Works in this collection was the approach to Sotheby's of 2008-08-14, and the earliest dated event in the auction engagement was the documentation session of 2008-08-08. This letter is dated 2008-08-11, so the name and the self-description as an artist and sole proprietor are now attested three days earlier than the approach to the trade. A further correction has since been required. This record previously said that the first documented outreach about the photograph was to a museum rather than to the trade, and that the museum was written to first. That is withdrawn. Two messages of 2008-08-08 to the Ansel Adams organisation precede this letter by two days, so the first documented outreach was to neither a museum nor the trade, and the order is: the Adams organisation, then the museum, then the auction house. What survives of the original point is narrower and still worth stating: the museum was approached before the auction house. The name Sun and Rain Works appears in neither earlier message, so this letter remains the earliest document carrying it. It is not a large interval, and both fall in the same week as the photographs." .
_:r29b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A sibling message of the same hour, and the first transmission header in this part of the collection. A further screenshot shows a message the studio forwarded on 2008-08-14 at 12:27 to a photographic-services address, carrying the same attachment, 'San Francisco.zip' at 3 MB. What it quotes beneath is not a rendered client view but a raw header block: sender, the subject 'Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco', and 'Date: Mon, 11 Aug 2008 16:01:02 -0700'. Three things in that line check out independently. 11 August 2008 was in fact a Monday. The offset of seven hours behind UTC is Pacific Daylight Time, which is what Oregon was observing in August, agreeing with the Eugene address the letters give. And the clock time of 16:01 is the same minute the museum letter's client view reports. The header is displayed rather than verified from a message file, so this is a stronger class of screenshot rather than a different class of evidence; but a raw Date line with a correct weekday and a correct seasonal offset is materially better than a client's rendering of a date." .
_:r29b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "That message is not the museum letter, and the difference should not be glossed. The museum letter opens 'Dear Sir or Madame'; this one opens with the first name of a named individual, and its body is shorter, omitting the paragraphs about ownership, research, the Portland appraisal contacts and the Adams institution. So the studio was sending the same subject line and the same attachment to more than one party within the same minute or two of the same afternoon, varying the salutation and the length. The header therefore corroborates the date, the hour and the timezone of the museum letter without being that letter's own header. Obtaining the museum letter as a message file was, when this passage was written, the thing that would verify it directly. That has since happened: the letter is now held as an exported message with its original Hotmail headers, and so is the sibling message, which quotes the letter's From, Subject and Date lines beneath its own and therefore attests the send time from a second file." .
_:r29b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The word 'happening', and how much weight it can bear. The message says: 'I thought that this happening might be of some interest to SF MOMA.' Two readings are available and this record does not choose between them. On the first, 'happening' is the ordinary English noun for an event or occurrence, and the sentence means no more than that the writer thought the situation might interest the museum. On the second, 'happening' is the term of art for a constructed, participatory art event, associated with Allan Kaprow and current in exactly the institutional context being written to, and the sentence offers the situation itself, rather than the object, as the thing of interest. The second reading is available because of who was being addressed: a modern art museum's photography department, by a writer who has just called himself an artist. It is not compelled, because the sentence works perfectly well under the first reading and nothing else in the message develops the idea. This is the closest thing to a contemporaneous conceptual framing of the situation that this collection holds, and it is a single ambiguous word in a letter otherwise concerned with attribution, appraisal and significance." .
_:r29b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The competing reading of the week as a whole, which should be weighed against the above. Within a few days in August 2008 the writer photographed the print, approached a museum, approached an auction house, sent the same letter and attachment to at least one further named individual and onward to a photographic-services address, reported contact with appraisers on the Portland art scene, and reported contact with a named person at an Adams institution. That is five or more parties in one week. That is the pattern of someone who believes he has found something valuable and is establishing what it is and what it is worth. The stated purpose in this letter is research into historical and artistic significance, and the appraisal contacts are about value. On that reading the approach to the museum is a request for expertise, which is an ordinary thing to ask a museum for, and carries no artistic intention at all. The breadth of the outreach supports this reading at least as well as the conceptual one, and a record that cited the word 'happening' without citing the surrounding behaviour would be selecting its evidence." .
_:r29b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r29b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Where the letter went. The photograph is of San Francisco, the subject line places the finding there ('Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco'), and the letter went to the modern art museum of that same city. Sun & Rain Works treats this as part of a deliberate framing, and the letter is consistent with that account. It does not discriminate between the two readings above, however: a person researching an Adams photograph of San Francisco would have had every reason to write to that city's museum of modern art first. What it does establish is narrower. The approach was addressed and composed rather than incidental - a chosen institution, a written headline, the same text sent to several parties within the week - which is evidence of deliberate outreach. It is not evidence of an intention, in 2008, to make the outreach an artwork, and should not be read as more." .
_:r29b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r29b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r2b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r2b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r2b1 .
_:r2b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P67i_is_referred_to_by> _:r2b2 .
_:r2b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E12_Production> .
_:r2b0 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Making of the Art Notes board" .
_:r2b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2010-05-20T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r2b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2012-11-30T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r2b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r2b1 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "between 20 May 2010 and 30 November 2012" .
_:r2b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Publicly posted from 2013-06-02. The same photograph is public on the studio's Flickr account, under the display name Joe Chirum and the user name LivingArt8, titled 'Art Notes' and marked 'Uploaded on June 2, 2013', at https://www.flickr.com/photos/24377024@N07/8921139312/ . The file there is pixel-identical to the studio's held original. So the board, and the line 'WTF is Art ?!' on it, stood at a publicly readable address on a third party's platform three months before the museum letter of 2013-09-12, with an upload date assigned by the platform rather than by the studio. That is the whole of the claim. The page records eight views in the thirteen years since, so nothing here says the line was read; only that it was posted, and dated by someone other than its author. Flickr's 'Taken on June 1, 2013' on that page is not a capture date: it is the value Flickr supplies when an uploaded file carries no Exif, and the copy uploaded had been stripped of it. The dating of the photograph itself is unchanged and is set out elsewhere in this record. The archived-websites record describes that account and the rest of the platform evidence." .
_:r2b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r2b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r2b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Dating, and what carries it. The board is evidenced by a single photograph held by the studio. That file's Exif, written by the camera, gives DateTimeOriginal, DateTimeDigitized and DateTime all as 2012:11:30 13:25:01, with no time zone recorded, and identifies the camera as a Canon PowerShot ELPH 100 HS, firmware 1.01, at f/2.8, 1/20s, ISO 250, 5.0mm, flash fired. The Canon MakerNote block survives intact at 3,640 bytes and its ImageType string reads 'IMG:PowerShot ELPH 100 HS JPEG'. The file carries no editing-software provenance: its only XMP is a single Windows-written xmp:Rating of 0, and there is no CreatorTool or ModifyDate. Two independent floors bracket the date from below and do not depend on the camera clock, which a user can set at will. First, the content of the board: the Paris museum theft the pinned New York Times report describes took place on the night of 19-20 May 2010, so the board as photographed cannot have been assembled before 20 May 2010, and the Christie's sale the other cutting reports took place on 4 May 2010. Second, the camera: the PowerShot ELPH 100 HS was announced on 7 February 2011 and shipped that March, so no file could have been made with it before 2011. The camera-written date of 30 November 2012 is consistent with both floors and is contradicted by nothing in the file. It remains a self-reported date on a studio-held file, of the same evidential kind as the rest of the private archive and weaker than the platform-assigned dates of the 2011-2012 postings; it is not independently notarized." .
_:r2b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r2b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r2b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Custody and publication. The photograph came to light in the studio's private source archive, on the same medium as the WTFIA folder described in the 2008-2009 evidence record; it is not one of the ninety-seven files that record enumerates, and it is later in date than all of them. The original as held is 4,000 x 3,000 pixels, 1,056,962 bytes, SHA-256 29c40767175e790b7ae443d3e020d27c13f645909811c59c95640721dd165081. What is published here is a display derivative, resampled to 1,600 x 1,200, 345,612 bytes, SHA-256 220b145dfa5464293edb91d0bbd5d2a1d9edc9bd99ab58f0768b66d0194bd320, carrying the camera's Exif block forward unaltered, including the MakerNote. The reduction is deliberate and is a rights measure, not a conservation one: at full resolution the body text of the two pinned press cuttings is readable, and at this size it is not, while the handwritten panel that is the work remains fully legible. The checksum of the original is published so the held file can later be shown to be the one described here without the full-resolution image being released." .
_:r2b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r2b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r2b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Scope. This record establishes that a board bearing the line 'WTF is Art ?!' and the name 'Sun and Rain Works' stood in the studio and was photographed on a date the file gives as 30 November 2012, no earlier than 20 May 2010 by its own content and no earlier than 2011 by its camera. It does not evidence the asserted 2007 origin of Metaconceptual Art, and it is not the earliest evidence in this collection: the 2008-2009 WTFIA folder is earlier in date, and the 2011-2012 postings remain the earliest evidence whose dates are assigned by a party other than the studio. The board's relation to the website www.wtfisart.com is one of shared wording, not of documented production: nothing here shows the board was made for the site or at the same time as it. No precedence over any other artist, work, or event is claimed from this record." .
_:r2b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r2b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r2b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A second work of the studio carries the same line. A camera-phone photograph dated 2011-11-08, described in the inscription-photograph record, shows a large handwritten sheet inscribed along its right edge '\"W.T.F. is Art ?\"', under a board carrying the phrase about the art historians of the future that the studio's museum letter of 2013-09-12 repeats. That sheet is a different object from this board: a different support, a different hand of the same writer, and different punctuation. It is dated nearly a year before the file that photographs this board, and the studio states that neither of those two physical works survives. This board is therefore not the only occurrence of the line in the studio's work, and it remains the earlier of the two to have reached a public platform." .
_:r2b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r2b14 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r2b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The panel's line about being the Art, and where the studio places it. The transcription above records the line '\"For the love of God, We are th[...]!\"', broken where the photograph stops being legible. The studio states, in 2026, that the full line ends 'We are the Art', and that it belongs to one deliberate chain with the sign-off 'For the Love of Art' used in its correspondence with the auction house in 2008 and 2009, and with the sentence 'For the Love of God, We are the Art. Long Live the Art Spirit !' that the Internet Archive captured on the studio's own website in 2014 and 2016. What the documents show is that all three exist and are dated in that order; that they were one design is the studio's account of its own intention and is recorded as that. The illegible tail of this line is not supplied by the studio's statement: the photograph still stops where it stops, and nothing here reads it." .
_:r2b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r2b15 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r2b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The board accreted rather than being made at one sitting, so the span is a bracket, not a date of completion. Its lower bound is the latest datable thing pinned to it, the Paris museum theft of 20 May 2010; its upper bound is the moment the only photograph of it was taken. Where within that span any given line was written is not recorded." .
_:r2b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r2b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r2b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r2b3 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Artwork page" .
_:r2b3 <https://linked.art/ns/terms/digitally_carried_by> _:r2b4 .
_:r2b4 <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/format> "text/html" .
_:r2b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.ics.forth.gr/isl/CRMdig/D1_Digital_Object> .
_:r2b4 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Metaconceptual Art — Art Notes" .
_:r2b4 <https://linked.art/ns/terms/access_point> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/artworks/art-notes> .
_:r2b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Art Notes" .
_:r2b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r2b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r2b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r2b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "work:art-notes" .
_:r2b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r2b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r2b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A framed studio notice board, half dry-erase panel and half cork, carrying handwritten notes in blue, black, red and green alongside pinned press cuttings and images. The written panel is headed 'Notes' over a drawn musical staff, and its first line reads 'WTF is Art ?!'. The same panel names 'Sun and Rain Works'. The board is the working surface on which the studio's questions were kept in view, and it is published here as a work in its own right: a note board about art that asks what art is, kept in the room where the work was made." .
_:r2b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r2b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r2b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Transcription of the handwritten panel, line by line as laid out: 'Notes' (heading, over a drawn treble staff, with three drawn notes); '\"WTF is Art ?!\"'; '\"There's a time and a place for now\"'; '\"We have Art today!\"'; '\"For the love of God, We are th[...]!\"'; '\"There is this little thing called the Universe, Art is a part of it\"'; '\"Sun and Rain Works\"'; '\"Alien Highways\"'; '\"Time is space, Space is Time, Love is Eternal\"'. A drawn heart sits at the lower left. One line is partly effaced by camera flare in the only photograph held, and its final two words cannot be read with confidence; the ellipsis marks that gap rather than an omission in the original. Every line is given in the quotation marks the writer used." .
_:r2b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r2b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r2b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Pinned to the cork half, as far as the photograph shows: a wire-service cutting headed 'Picasso fetches record price', reporting the sale of the 1932 painting 'Nude, Green Leaves and Bust' for $106.5 million at Christie's, annotated in ink in the margin with a date in the form '5-10'; a New York Times report by Doreen Carvajal headed 'Picasso, Matisse nabbed in theft at Paris museum', on the theft of five paintings from the Paris Museum of Modern Art, valued in the report at $114 million to $127 million; a cut-out reading 'Full Circle' beside a fragment reading 'PRINT THIS'; an illustrated street map of Lower Manhattan hand-lettered 'LOWER HEDGISTAN' with Broadway and Wall Street named, credited in the caption to Leif Parsons; a photograph of the Earth from space; a bald eagle's eye; a satellite image of cloud and coastline; and a detail of a hundred-dollar bill overlaid with two photographic portraits. The two press cuttings are third-party copyright material, incidental to the work and reproduced here only at a size at which their body text is not legible." .
_:r2b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r2b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: the Sotheby's consignment correspondence (2008-2009)" .
_:r30b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r30b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r30b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r30b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-sothebys-approach-2008" .
_:r30b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r30b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r30b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The attachment as held. 'San Francisco.zip', 3,553,357 bytes, SHA-256 227328da2ffe06ef140d79515499ddf1451a5669abaf20c9f9e70d384ed36c43. It contains twelve entries: the eleven documentation frames DSCF0012 to DSCF0017 and DSCF0020 to DSCF0024, and a Windows thumbnail cache. Frames 0018 and 0019 are absent, the same gap the documentation-photograph record notes in the studio's loose copies. Every one of the twelve is byte-for-byte identical to the corresponding file in the studio's private source archive, so the archive's copies and the transmitted copies are the same data. The archive carries Info-ZIP UNIX extra fields, header id 0x5855, holding access and modification times as Unix epoch integers; these decode to 2008-08-08 07:05:36Z through 07:10:24Z for the eleven frames. Those are unambiguous UTC values, unlike the local-time fields a zip normally carries, and they sit seven hours after the camera's own Exif clock, an offset equal to Pacific Daylight Time, the zone of the Eugene, Oregon address the message gives." .
_:r30b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Four independent agreements between the message and the photographs, each checkable. The message describes the attached photographs as made with a 1.3 megapixel FujiFilm camera; the frames carry camera-written Exif naming a FUJIFILM FinePix1300 and measure 1280 by 960 pixels, which is 1.23 megapixels, the rating the model is sold under. The message shows an attachment of 3 MB; the eleven frames and the thumbnail cache occupy 3,553,357 bytes, which a mail client displays as 3 MB. The message is dated 2008-08-14; the frames are dated 2008-08-08, six days earlier. The attachment is named for San Francisco; the lot is titled 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks'. This is the first point in the collection at which camera-written metadata on a studio-held file is corroborated by a separate document rather than only by itself. The corroboration is not independent of the studio, since both artifacts come from it, but it is independent of the files: the caveat that Exif can in principle be altered would now require the message to have been fabricated to match it." .
_:r30b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the archive cannot do. A zip file carries the timestamps of the files put into it, not a record of when it was made, so this one cannot date its own creation and does not by itself establish that anything was sent in 2008. Its container fingerprint is worth recording because it is unexpected: create-system 3 with Info-ZIP UNIX extra fields means the archive was written by Info-ZIP tooling on a Unix-like system, not by the Windows compressed-folder command, although the thumbnail cache inside it shows the source folder was a Windows one. Whether that reflects the machine used in 2008 or a later repackaging is not determinable from the file. The honest position is that the attachment corroborates the content of the message and the dating of the photographs, and that the date of transmission rests on the client display alone until the message itself is examined." .
_:r30b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The Warhol frame was transmitted. DSCF0020, exposed at 00:08:46 by the camera's clock, shows a copy of 'The Philosophy of Andy Warhol (From A to B and Back Again)' stood upright and facing the camera against the framed print. The documentation-photograph record already described this frame and read it as the earliest dated trace of the thinking behind the concept of the auction as artwork. The attachment establishes something further about it: the frame was not kept in the studio. It went to the auction house, inside the set of photographs attached to the first approach. The message says nothing about the book. So what is evidenced is that an art-historical reference was deliberately composed into documentation sent to Sotheby's, and left unremarked. That is a gesture made outward rather than a private note, which is more than the record could previously say. It remains short of a designation: the message frames the print as property being researched, not as material of a work, and the auction that the designation concerns did not yet exist in August 2008." .
_:r30b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The catalogue photograph, held by the studio. Among the files recovered in 2026 from a USB flash drive the studio used with its Macs is 'san francisco.jpg', 1,430 by 1,198 pixels, 200,688 bytes, SHA-256 ac6d1aac01ed51467b575ed11a850bd8b9ef85586bf6ea2756eca411612190ea. It is a copy-stand photograph of the matted print, taken square-on beside a grey-scale colour strip of the kind used in catalogue photography, and the drive's directory entry gives it the date 2009-04-01 07:11:04, two days after the sale. That date is carried by the file from the studio's own machine, so it is the studio's account, not the auction house's. The photograph was compared on 2026-09-24 with the image Sotheby's publishes today for this lot, catalogue sale N08533, lot 16, the source file 'n08533-16-lr-1.jpg', 2,000 by 2,000 pixels, SHA-256 a6830325b73a8450a9425e7b0e81678c15256da3833db9d956129713e67d8207. Aligned by 2,094 matched image features, the two are related by a pure enlargement of 1.933 and a shift, with no rotation, no perspective difference, and a median error of 0.16 of a pixel. Two separate photographs of the same print do not align that exactly, so the two files are one photograph. Neither was made from the other. Sotheby's file is a tighter crop of the print alone and carries markedly more fine detail than the studio's, while the studio's shows more of the frame, the whole mat and the colour strip, than Sotheby's does. Both therefore descend from a larger, uncropped original, which, since the auction house publishes it as its catalogue image of the lot, is taken here to be Sotheby's own photograph. The studio states, in 2026, that Sotheby's sent it the image. No message carrying it is among those held: the image is not attached to any of the thread's messages that the studio has produced as files, so the sending itself is the studio's account. What the comparison adds is independent of that account: the studio's file is the auction house's catalogue photograph, in a fuller frame than the one it publishes, and not a photograph the studio took, which is consistent with the studio's account of how it was obtained. The photograph is not reproduced here, because the rights in it are the auction house's; its checksum is published so that the held file can be matched. It says nothing about the sale as an artwork: it is the auction house's record of the object it sold." .
_:r30b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b14 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Redaction and scope. Following this collection's convention, the Sotheby's specialist addressed in the message is identified by department and role only; their name and mailbox are withheld pending consent, as are the studio principal's then-current personal mailbox and telephone number, all of which are retained in the studio's private copy. The sothebys.com domain is deliberately retained, because the fact of an approach to that institution is the point of the record. The recipient institution was asked in 2026 for its copy of this correspondence and replied that it could not locate any; see the note on the outcome of the sale. What this record establishes is an approach, an identity, and a set of photographs. It establishes no acceptance, valuation, or endorsement by Sotheby's, no consignor of record, and nothing about the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection." .
_:r30b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b15 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b16 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The thread continues to an executed contract. On 2009-03-18 the department sent a scanned consignment agreement, stating that a signed agreement was required before the photograph could be sold, and on 2009-03-19 the studio returned it signed as a PDF attachment. That file is held. It is described, checksummed and deliberately left unpublished in the consignment agreement record, which also sets out the two reasons it cannot be published: a confidentiality undertaking in the agreement itself, and a page of government identity documentation inside it." .
_:r30b16 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b16 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b17 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The business was disclosed at first contact, and what that does and does not carry. The opening message to the auction house, of 2008-08-14, introduces its writer, in its first two sentences and before it reaches the photograph, as an artist living in Eugene, Oregon and as 'the sole proprietor of a fledgeling Art business, Sun and Rain Works'. So the auction house was told at the outset, in the same message that raised the print, that it was dealing with a working art business and with the person who was that business. Three consequences follow, and they should be kept apart. First, the name Sun and Rain Works is attached to this engagement from its first minute, not applied to it afterwards. Second, because a sole proprietorship has no legal personality separate from its proprietor, the consignment agreement executed in the proprietor's own name seven months later is the agreement of that business, made in the only name in which such a business can contract; the earlier statement in this collection that the contract evidences an individual rather than a business drew a distinction this business form does not support, and has been corrected. Third, and against the grain of the first two, the message does not say that the business is consigning anything. No consignment existed on 2008-08-14: the auction house made its offer the following day and the studio accepted a month after that. What the message states about the property is personal ownership and research. The business is the writer's declared identity in the exchange, not a stated party to a transaction that had not yet been proposed. One word in that self-description is recorded here rather than left for someone else to find. The business is called 'a fledgeling Art business' in August 2008, while the market's membership record places the same business at stalls in Eugene in 2005, 2006 and 2008, and this collection dates its founding to 2005. A concern three years old may reasonably call itself fledgeling, and small trading businesses commonly do, so the two statements are not in contradiction. They are in tension only to the extent that a reader expecting a settled enterprise will not find one described here, and the word is the studio's own, written at the time, to a party it was trying to impress. It is set down for the same reason the market record sets down the missing year of 2007: a reader comparing the documents will see it, and a record that passed over it would be less honest than one that does not." .
_:r30b17 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b17 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b18 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The estimate agrees with the published catalogue, and anyone can check it. In the offer of 2008-08-15 the cataloguer proposed a provisional estimate of $7,000 to $10,000. The public catalogue entry for lot 16 of sale N08533, published some seven months later, carries an estimate of $7,000 to $10,000. That is a figure quoted in private correspondence before the catalogue existed, matching the figure the auction house later printed. It is the second point at which this studio-held correspondence can be tested against the public record without access to the archive, the first being the agreement of the verso markings in the 2008 photographs with the catalogue's physical description. The catalogue also prints the negative number 1-SF-46 and the American Trust Company commission, both of which appear in the studio's documentation." .
_:r30b18 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b18 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b19 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The outcome of the sale, now established by the auction house's own document. This collection previously recorded the result as an open question of fact, because the public catalogue page places it behind an account login. On 2026-09-25, in reply to the studio's records request of the same day, Sotheby's Prints and Photographs seller operations, identified here by role only, sent the studio principal a copy of the auction house's consignor statement for this sale, marked 'Duplicate'. The statement gives sale N08533, Photographs, a sale date and a document date (tax point) of 30 March 2009, and US dollars as the sale currency. It is addressed to 'Mr Joseph Chirum' and issued under a Sotheby's client account in that name. Its single line is lot 0016, Ansel Adams, 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks', marked 'Sold', at a hammer price of 9,000 US dollars, within the pre-sale estimate of 7,000 to 10,000 US dollars that the auction house gave in 2008 and that the catalogue entry repeats. The hammer price is the auctioneer's winning bid and excludes the buyer's premium, so it is not the sum any buyer paid. The statement also itemises the seller's commission and charges and the net amount due. Following the convention of the consignment agreement record, those figures are not given here, and neither are the account number, the document number or the postal address. The file is a PDF of 72,670 bytes, SHA-256 9706f42179283f56d98283f9c650ae1aeea837132a7187929d913e630bd1fa66: a covering letter dated 2026-09-25, the one-page statement, and a blank third page. It is a present-day reprint from the auction house's accounting records of 2009, not a paper of 2009, and it is not published. What it settles: the lot sold, and the auction house's own records name the studio principal as its consignor. What it does not do: it names no buyer, and none was sought; it does not name Sun & Rain Works, consistent with a sole proprietor consigning in his own name; and it says nothing about the sale as an artwork. In the same reply the auction house said it was unable to locate the consignment agreement or any other correspondence with the studio. Its copies of the August 2008 messages, the March 2009 messages and the signed agreement are therefore not known to survive, and the studio's copies are the only ones held. A public copy of the result also exists, and it can be checked without the statement. Sotheby's own web page for sale N08533 was captured by the Internet Archive on 2019-07-19 at 07:10:18 UTC, at https://web.archive.org/web/20190719071018/http://www.sothebys.com/en/auctions/2009/photographs-n08533.html , a capture of 45,818 bytes. It gives the sale total as 2,384,690 US dollars for 169 lots, and the lot data embedded in the page record lot 16, 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks' by Ansel Adams, under the auction house's item identifier 3QXGW, with an estimate of 7,000 to 10,000 US dollars, as sold, at a sale price of 11,250 US dollars. The item identifier is the one printed against lot 0016 on the consignor statement, so the lot on the public page and the lot on the studio's statement are the same entry in the auction house's records. The published price is the statement's hammer price plus 25 per cent, which is what a buyer's premium at that rate on a hammer price of 9,000 US dollars produces, so the two figures agree. Like the catalogue, the page names no consignor and no buyer." .
_:r30b19 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b19 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status, restated after the messages were produced as files. Until 2026-09-20 every message in this thread was held only as a screenshot of a mail client, and this record said so and reported the thread rather than verifying it. Five of the studio's outbound messages are now held as message files exported from its own mailbox: the approach of 2008-08-14, the acceptance of 2008-09-16, the message of 2008-11-20, and the two of March 2009 that carry the consignment sheet and the executed agreement. Every phrase this record quotes has been checked against them and agrees word for word. Three limits stand. The files are the studio's own outbound copies, so the department's replies are held only as text quoted beneath them and no separately held copy of any reply exists here. They carry no Received chain, which is ordinary for Sent Items copies but means they are not transit records. And the auction house has not been asked to confirm anything. What they do carry is set out in the next passage, and it is stronger than anything this collection held before." .
_:r30b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b20 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "An earlier letter of the same week has since been produced, and it displaces part of what this record says about firsts. On 2008-08-11, three days before the approach to Sotheby's and under an identical subject line, the studio wrote to the photography department of the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art, repeating the same opening paragraph almost word for word. The earliest dated use of the name Sun and Rain Works in this collection is therefore that letter, not this one. The further claim once made here, that the first documented outreach about the photograph was to a museum rather than to the trade, is withdrawn: two messages of 2008-08-08 to the Ansel Adams organisation precede the museum letter by two days, so the first documented outreach was to the organisation bearing the photographer's name, and the museum was second and this letter third. This record remains the first approach to the auction house." .
_:r30b20 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b20 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b21 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The artist's account of the concealment, recorded as his account and given in 2026. Sun & Rain Works states that the work was conceived under the influence of the Merry Pranksters, and that disclosing a conceptual framing to the auction house would have destroyed the form: the counterparty's unawareness is, on this account, part of the work rather than a gap in it. The proposition is coherent and it is locally grounded, since Ken Kesey farmed at Pleasant Hill, some fifteen miles from the Eugene market where the studio was trading in the same years. It also disposes of an argument that appeared in earlier versions of these records: the absence, across the trade correspondence, of any statement that the consignment was a work carries little weight, because that correspondence is with a cataloguer and an administrator about estimates, deadlines and signatures, and is not where such a framing would be put in any case. Two limits are recorded alongside it. The account is testimony given seventeen years after the events and is not contemporaneous evidence of what was intended in 2008 or 2009. And the model it invokes cuts both ways: the Pranksters concealed the prank from its subject while documenting it obsessively for themselves, so an appeal to that lineage explains the silence toward Sotheby's without explaining the silence in the studio's own contemporaneous papers, where the WTFIA folder of ninety-seven files, worked on until the week before the sale, never mentions the consignment. That internal absence remains the open question, and no account of the external one reaches it." .
_:r30b21 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b21 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b22 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Two further messages extend the thread through the autumn of 2008. On 2008-09-17, on a raw header reading 'Wed, 17 Sep 2008 09:45:03 -0400', the cataloguer writes that there is no rush, that the deadline is January 2009, that cataloguing for the April sale begins in November, and that she will put the consignor in touch with the department's administrator, whom she names, because a code is required for the item to be admitted to the building and unsolicited packages are refused. She adds a personal line about having holidayed in the islands when her family lived in California. On 2008-11-20 the consignor replies, opening 'Aloha Lauren', that he will be in contact soon 'regarding the shipment of Ansel Adams from Twin Peaks in SanFrancisco to Sotheby's in NYC for the upcoming spring Art auction'. The phrase takes the print's title, 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks', and gives it to its maker: it is Ansel Adams, not the photograph, who is to be shipped from Twin Peaks, whether as shorthand or as wordplay. He is 'somewhat unsure' whether to keep the photograph in its original frame with its glass cover 'or have a professional art framer do the removal of the glass for increased safety and decreased likelihood of damage to the piece during shipment', says he can arrange either 'in the next week or two', and closes the paragraph: 'I look forward to talking with you further about this art in progress.' He writes that he is 'still on the islands, on Maui', and gives a post-office box there as his mailing address. The mail client displays the message as sent on Thu 11/20/2008 at 4:49 PM; the same client renders the museum letter of 2008-08-11 at UTC minus eight, so this is 00:49 UTC on 21 November. An earlier version of this record paraphrased the message as concerning the shipment of the photograph 'from San Francisco to New York'; the message's own words are quoted here instead. The header's weekday and its offset of four hours behind UTC, which is Eastern Daylight Time in September, are both correct." .
_:r30b22 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b22 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b23 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Two identifications the thread now settles. The administrator whose first name appears in the offer letter of 2008-08-15, and who chases the signed agreement in March 2009, is named in full by the cataloguer on 2008-09-17, so the same member of staff runs through the correspondence from the first offer to the executed contract. And the Maui post-office box given on 2008-11-20 is the Hawai'i mailing address that the auction-engagement record describes as linking the consignor to the studio's domain and hosting receipts. That link had rested on the studio's own account of its papers; it is now visible in a message to the auction house. The box number itself is withheld here, as personal address data, and retained in the studio's private copy." .
_:r30b23 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b23 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b24 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The register of the correspondence, weighed as a whole. The claim that this thread is purely commercial cannot be sustained in its strongest form. Across the eleven messages now known, and to three different recipients, the consignor's own language is persistently art-inflected: the museum letter of 2008-08-11 offers 'this happening' as something that might interest the institution; the approach to the auction house closes 'For the Love of Art'; the subject line under which the exchange with the auction house ran is 'Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco', the same one used for the museum, and the auction house's replies carry it forward, though by the ordinary mechanics of replying rather than by any choice of theirs; the acceptance of 2008-09-16 speaks of consigning 'the Art', of 'the shipment of the art' and of 'this project'; the message of 2008-11-20 speaks of 'the upcoming spring Art auction', of 'a professional art framer' and of 'this art in progress'; the covering note returning the signed agreement in March 2009 allows an exception to saving paper 'for the purposes of Art' and signs off 'Yours in Art'. That is a run of such phrases over seven months, in a consistent voice, and it is not the voice of a person disposing of an asset. A limit on part of it: in 'consigning the Art' and 'the shipment of the art' the word names the print itself, and an Ansel Adams photograph is a work of art in the ordinary sense, so those two phrases do not distinguish sending an artwork to auction from making one; the auction house, answering in the same exchange, calls the object 'your Ansel Adams photograph'. To them should be added an act rather than a phrase: the signed consignment agreement was returned in a file named for the consignor with the word Art standing where his middle name belongs, which the consignment agreement record treats separately. What it is not, equally, is a designation. Every one of these is a turn of phrase inside a message about appraisal, shipping, glass removal or file formats. They are also not of equal weight, and a reader citing this record should know which sentence is carrying it. The opening declarations of 2008-08-14, that the writer is an artist and the sole proprietor of an art business, establish who was writing and nothing beyond it: an artist who owns a photograph and sends it to auction is doing what artists who own photographs ordinarily do, and those two sentences would read the same if no work were intended. 'Consigning the Art' and 'the shipment of the art' fare no better, for the reason given above. 'The upcoming spring Art auction', 'a professional art framer' and 'Yours in Art' are register rather than statement. What remains, and what this record now treats as the load-bearing phrase of the whole thread, is 'this art in progress', written on 2008-11-20, some four months before the sale. It adapts the fixed phrase 'work in progress', which means a work being made, by putting 'art' where 'work' belongs, the same substitution the studio made in the filename of the signed agreement and in the sign-off adapted from Hirst; read that way, it most naturally describes something being made rather than a matter being administered. An earlier version of this passage called the phrase at least as readable in the ordinary sense, an art matter currently under way; that sense remains available, but it is the weaker reading. Three limits hold it short of a designation, and they should travel with any citation of it. First, 'in progress' is ambiguous in ordinary English between a thing being made and a matter under way: a painting in progress is being made, a meeting in progress is not, and which sense applies turns on whether the head noun names something built or something that unfolds. 'Art' is both, which is why the phrase is suggestive and also why it does not settle the question. Second, the demonstrative has no secure antecedent, though the message now held as a file narrows the field considerably. The sentence runs: 'I will be able to arrange this either way in the next week or two, so I look forward to talking with you further about this art in progress.' It opens a paragraph of its own; the frame, the glass cover and the framer are discussed in the paragraph before it. So the candidates are not, as this record previously said, the photograph and its frame and glass and shipment sitting alongside the phrase in one paragraph. The sentence carries two demonstratives, and the first of them, 'arrange this either way', takes the framing decision. The second cannot take the same thing without redundancy, and the 'so' between them subordinates the framing to whatever the art in progress is. What remains open is whether that second 'this' reaches the print, the consignment, or something the writer did not name; if it reaches the print, the stronger reading still fails, because an Ansel Adams photograph of 1952 is finished art and not art in the making. Third, the phrase does not say what the work in progress is: the auction, the print's passage to it, or the website whose material the studio was assembling in the same months. A fourth limit stood here until 2026-09-20 and is now withdrawn. It said that the phrase, like every other message in this thread, was held only as a screenshot, so that the strongest contemporaneous phrase in this collection was also among its least hardened, and that exporting the message would change this. The message has since been exported. It carries a provider-stamped X-Originating-IP, a Date with a real local offset, and a References chain whose identifiers were written by the auction house's own mail server. The phrase is now among the best evidenced things here rather than the least. Two corrections of setting go with that. An earlier version of this passage placed the phrase inside the sentence about removing glass from a frame; a second placed it at the close of the paragraph discussing the glass. Both were wrong, both made the setting sound more administrative than it is, and both were written from a paraphrase rather than from the message. Two considerations hold the phrase up against those limits, and they are not of the same kind. The writer uses ordinary words for the print in the paragraph immediately before, 'the photograph' and 'the piece', so reaching for 'this art in progress' in the next breath is a marked choice against a vocabulary he had just been using. And the substitution is not isolated: the same person, in the same engagement, across seven months, put 'art' into 'work in progress', 'Art' into Hirst's 'For the Love of God', and 'Art' into his own name in the filename of the executed consignment agreement. Any one of the three is deniable as idiom or accident. The three together are a habit of composition showing in the documentary record, and that pattern, rather than the literal force of any single phrase, is the strongest thing this thread offers. The honest position is that the correspondence carries a sustained artistic self-presentation, one phrase within it that points past self-presentation toward making, and no statement that the auction is a work. All three parts of that should be reported by anyone citing this record." .
_:r30b24 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b24 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b25 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The sign-off 'For the Love of Art', and the studio's account of it. The studio's account, given in 2026, is that the sign-off was chosen deliberately, as its own adaptation of the title of Damien Hirst's For the Love of God, the diamond-set platinum cast of a skull first exhibited in London in June 2007 with a widely reported asking price of £50 million, and not as a stock valediction. The objective features are consistent with that account. The phrase is that title with one word changed, 'Art' standing where 'God' belongs: the same substitution of the word Art into a fixed form that this record notes in 'this art in progress' and in the filename of the signed agreement. The work it adapts had, fourteen months earlier, made its price a subject of wide press coverage, and the letters carrying the sign-off concerned the appraisal and sale of a work of art. And the studio later used the unaltered title in its own material: the handwritten notes panel recorded in this collection carries 'For the love of God, We are th[...]!', and the captures of www.wtfisart.com of 2014 and 2016 read 'For the Love of God, We are the Art.' So the link between the phrase and Hirst's title is one the studio demonstrably made. Against that, and as plainly: neither message of August 2008 names Hirst or the skull; 'for the love of' is an ordinary English idiom; nobody receiving the messages was invited to see a reference; and the later uses show the link was made, not that it was made by 2008. The account of intent is the studio's own, given eighteen years afterwards, and is reported here as that account, not as a fact the messages establish. What the messages do establish is narrower: the sign-off closes both surviving approaches of August 2008, to two institutions three days apart, and 'Yours in Art' closes the note of March 2009, so it was a settled signature of the correspondence rather than a one-off." .
_:r30b25 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b25 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b26 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The Warhol frame, and a loop that closes inside the studio's own contemporaneous papers. The volume composed with the print in DSCF0020, and transmitted with the other ten frames to a museum on 2008-08-11 and to the auction house on 2008-08-14, is 'The Philosophy of Andy Warhol (From A to B and Back Again)'. That book is the source of the best-known statement of art as commerce: its Business Art section runs 'I wanted to be an art business man or a business artist. Being good in business is the most fascinating kind of art', and continues 'making money is art and working is art and good business is the best art'. Two things follow that are matters of record rather than interpretation. First, the letters carrying those photographs introduce their writer, in their second sentence, as the sole proprietor of an art business - the position Warhol's passage names. Second, five months later the studio compiled into 'IsItArt.doc' a press article quoting that same dictum in full. So the book was owned and deliberately staged in August 2008, the self-description in the covering letters matches its central idea, and the idea reappears in the studio's own research file in January 2009. The engagement with that specific thesis is documented across the period rather than asserted about it." .
_:r30b26 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b26 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b27 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the Warhol frame is, and what it still is not. The studio's account is that the book was placed as an obvious self-reference. The objective features support a deliberate gesture rather than incidental clutter: the volume is propped upright and square to the camera on the same ground as the print, in one frame of eleven, and the set was sent onward to two art institutions. Read against the transaction it accompanies, pairing the object being consigned to an auction house with the canonical text on art as business is precise rather than decorative, and the record of this collection identified that frame as the earliest dated trace of the thinking behind the concept of the auction as artwork before the studio said anything about it. Against that: the gesture was unremarked in every message, so nobody at either institution was invited to read it, and a cataloguer receiving eleven photographs of a print would reasonably see a book. Whether an unremarked gesture is a communication or a private signature is exactly the question the studio's account of concealment raises, and this record does not settle it. What it can say is that the gesture is deliberate, dated, transmitted, and thematically exact." .
_:r30b27 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b27 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b28 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The archive was not first sent here. San Francisco.zip, whose SHA-256 is published above as the 3 MB attachment to the message of 2008-08-14, is byte-identical to the attachment of a message sent to the Ansel Adams organisation on the evening of 2008-08-08. The same archive therefore went to the organisation bearing the photographer's name six days before it went to the trade, and existed in its final form within a day of the photographs being made. Nothing about the auction engagement turns on this, but any account of the archive that began with this message would be starting in the middle." .
_:r30b28 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b28 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b29 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The filename is attested in the sender's own words, on a dated message. This collection treats JoeArtChirum.pdf, the name under which the executed consignment agreement was returned, as an act rather than a phrase: the word Art standing where the consignor's middle name belongs. Until the message files were produced, that gesture rested on a file held on the studio's own media. It now rests on the covering message of 2009-03-19 as well, which says 'I have attached a pdf file: JoeArtChirum.pdf' in its body, allows an exception to saving paper 'for the purposes of Art', and closes 'Yours in Art'. The attachment on that message is byte-identical to the held file, SHA-256 970c8e85bf0931ac8bfed1ab742d7f67247cdb2003c4db4aea99b9b29138eabe, which this collection had already published. So the naming is contemporaneous, deliberate enough to be typed out in prose, and dated by a message whose headers were written in 2009. What it still is not is a designation of the auction as a work, and the message says nothing of the kind." .
_:r30b29 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b29 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The auction house's own mail server corroborates the thread. The messages of 2008-11-20 and March 2009 carry In-Reply-To and References headers, and those headers hold eleven message identifiers generated by NYCEXC01.ads.sothebys.com, which is the auction house's own Exchange server, alongside identifiers generated by the sending provider at phx.gbl. Nothing in this collection produced those eleven strings. They were written by the receiving institution when it replied, and ordinary mail threading copied them verbatim into the studio's next message. Two unrelated systems therefore attest the same exchange, which is the same form of corroboration the 2013 museum correspondence rests on, where identifiers written by one provider reappear in a university server's headers. It is the strongest corroboration in this collection, and unlike everything else here it is checkable by a party other than the studio: anyone with access to the auction house's archive can look those identifiers up. Two things it is not. It is not a statement by the auction house about this project or about Metaconceptual Art, and no endorsement by it is implied or should be inferred. And it corroborates that the exchange happened, on the dates and in the order recorded; it says nothing about what the consignment was." .
_:r30b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b30 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r30b30 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r30b31 .
_:r30b30 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r30b30 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the 2008 approach record" .
_:r30b31 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r30b31 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r30b31 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r30b31 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r30b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The thread, message by message. 2008-08-14, studio to Sotheby's: the approach described above, with the zip attached. 2008-08-15, Sotheby's to studio, opening 'As per our recent conversation': an offer to include the Adams 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks' in the April 2009 sale at a provisional estimate of $7,000 to $10,000, 'bearing in mind that it is in perfect condition'; a deadline of the end of January 2009; an instruction that the print be arranged through the department before sending, since unsolicited packages are turned away; the first name of the administrator who would supply the forms; and an attachment of consignment charges. The phrase 'as per our recent conversation' records a telephone call between the two messages of which nothing else survives. 2008-09-15, Sotheby's to studio, on a header timestamped 13:56:41 -0400 and under the subject 'RE: Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco': a follow-up about 'your Ansel Adams photograph', asking whether the owner still wishes to consign it to the April 2009 auction. 2008-09-16, studio to Sotheby's, under the same subject: 'I am interested in consigning the Art in the April 2009 auction'; a request for the deadlines; a note that the writer is visiting Hawai'i at that time; an undertaking to 'arrange for the shipment of the art myself'; and thanks for the 'reminder of this project'. The client view of that reply shows it as sent Tue 9/16/2008 at 1:55 PM, records that it was forwarded on 2024-06-05, and quotes the 2008-09-15 message beneath it with its raw header lines." .
_:r30b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the auction house's own words settle. In the 2008-08-15 and 2008-09-15 messages Sotheby's names the object by the exact title under which it was later catalogued as lot 16 of sale N08533, values it, and proposes to sell it for the person it is writing to. That is the auction house, not the studio, attesting the consignment relationship. Taken with the documentation photographs, the verso agreement with the public catalogue entry, and the transmitted attachment, the consignment ceases to be a matter of the studio's account. Two further details follow from the thread. The writer's presence in Hawai'i in September 2008 agrees with the Hawai'i mailing address that links the consignor to the studio's domain records. And the sale was planned throughout this correspondence as an April 2009 sale, whereas N08533 was held on 30 March 2009 - a schedule that moved, and a fact that bears on the misdated month in the 2013 museum letter, which the correspondence record treats separately." .
_:r30b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the thread does not contain. In none of its messages, though the studio had every occasion to say so, does the thread state that the consignment or the sale is an artwork, a performance, or a conceptual project. The word 'project' does occur once, in the acceptance of 2008-09-16, which thanks the auction house for its 'reminder of this project'; the reminder it answers was a query about consigning the print, so the word names the consignment matter without saying what kind of project it is. The auction house's side is ordinary trade throughout: estimates, deadlines, forms and shipping. The studio's side deals with the same practical matters in a vocabulary of its own, set out in the note on the register of the correspondence below: 'the Art', 'the upcoming spring Art auction', 'a professional art framer', 'this art in progress', 'Yours in Art'. Earlier versions of this record said that nothing in the thread frames the consignment as an artwork, that its only art-inflected touches were 'the Art' and a sign-off, and that it is not evidence that the engagement was framed as art at the time. All three statements are withdrawn as too strong. The thread is strong evidence of the consignment, of the studio's identity as a working art business, and of the studio presenting the engagement as art while it was under way. It is not a statement that the auction itself was a work, which is the designation this collection records: the messages of March 2009 fall within three weeks of the sale, but they are administrative, a chase for a signed agreement, a reply about post, and the return of the signed contract. The earliest explicit statement in this collection that the sale was a work is a private message of 2010-04-12, dated by Facebook's servers, in which the studio principal calls the sale 'an act of contemporary conceptual art', thirteen months after the sale; the earliest delivered to an institution is the museum letter of 2013." .
_:r30b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The consignment agreement, March 2009. On 2009-03-11, nineteen days before the sale, the administrator of the Photographs department writes from a sothebys.com address, on a header timestamped 'Wed, 11 Mar 2009 12:01:52 -0400', under the subject 'in regards to consignment agreement for SOTHEBY'S'. She says the catalogue should have arrived and that she hopes the recipient is pleased with the presentation of 'your photograph'; that the sale is rapidly approaching and no signed consignment agreement has come back; that she posted it at the very beginning of February; and that she will issue a new one if needed. Her signature block carries the department's sale line, 'Photographs March 30, 2009'. The following day the studio replies that the consignment sheet was received but the catalogue was not, that the sheets will be faxed, and that post is reaching a general-delivery address in Eugene because forwarding to Maui has been slow. The administrator who writes here is the member of staff the 2008 offer letter said would supply the consignment forms, so the two ends of the thread name the same person." .
_:r30b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The consignor of record. This collection has until now recorded that the identity of the consignor was not established, on the ground that the published catalogue names no consignor, as is standard auction practice. That caveat can now be narrowed. In these two messages the auction house's own Photographs department, writing to the studio principal's mailbox nineteen days before the sale, calls the object 'your photograph', refers to 'your catalogue' and to 'your consignment agreement', and chases the signed agreement it had posted in February; the studio answers that it holds the consignment sheet and will return it. A consignment agreement is the instrument by which a consignor consigns. Two things should still be said. When this note was written the signed agreement was not held, so what it evidenced was the auction house treating this person as its consignor rather than the executed contract. The agreement has since been recovered and has its own record, and the auction house's own sale statement now names the studio principal as consignor; see the note on the outcome of the sale. And the catalogue remains silent on the point, so a reader working only from the public record still cannot see it. That is why the statements elsewhere in this collection that the catalogue does not name Sun & Rain Works are correct, and they are left standing." .
_:r30b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r30b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Location, across the whole run of evidence. The 2008 opening message gives Eugene, Oregon. The market membership record places the same business at stalls in Eugene in 2005, 2006 and 2008. In September 2008 the studio writes from Hawai'i. In March 2009 it is receiving post at a general delivery address in Eugene while mail is being forwarded, slowly, to Maui. The Hawai'i mailing address that links the consignor to the studio's domain records is on Maui. These are four documents, two of them from third parties, describing one person moving between the same two places across four years, and they agree with each other. Personal addresses are withheld here." .
_:r30b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r30b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r31b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: the staged photographs of the print (autumn 2008)" .
_:r31b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r31b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r31b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r31b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-staged-photographs-2008" .
_:r31b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r31b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r31b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the staging can and cannot carry. The studio's account is that these are theatrical photographs, made by its art assistants as documentation of WTF is Ansel Adams. The frames support more of that account than any earlier document in this collection. The storage frames are directed rather than incidental: a pose at a door with the keys held up to the lens, the print lifted in the doorway and then presented squarely to the camera, the same subject arranged in several settings. They stage a finding, and the subject line under which the studio had approached a museum and an auction house three months earlier was 'Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco'. Five days before the Mac's date on these files, the studio described the matter to the auction house as 'this art in progress'. The ordinary reading is also available and should be stated with equal force. A person who has written that he owns a previously unknown piece, and who is preparing to consign it, has every reason to photograph where it was kept, its condition, its verso, and its removal from the frame for shipment; the verso frames and the workshop frames are documentation of exactly that kind, and the exterior frames record a place. On that reading the posed frames are a record of the retrieval, however it was staged. This record does not choose between the readings. It records that the series is staged, that it was made before the sale, and that it was kept inside the studio." .
_:r31b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r31b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r31b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What this record does not show. None of these frames was among the photographs sent to the museum on 2008-08-11 or to the auction house on 2008-08-14, which were the eleven frames of 2008-08-08, and nothing in this collection shows that anyone outside the studio saw them in 2008 or 2009. They are not evidence of a public act, or of the auction house's knowledge of any artistic framing. They carry no statement, caption or title that designates the retrieval or the sale as a work. They say nothing about the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection. And their dates bound the files' processing, not their capture." .
_:r31b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r31b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r31b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Checksums of the files as held, SHA-256, in frame order, with sizes in bytes. P1010001.jpg, 74069, 6f6f8a275f3abeefb6367c8957113da5f56af2f56b72a74ed43ef426f3a0d07f. P1010002.jpg, 175808, bc0230d510b6215f213948126387604e06145fd3bff711387d6b406e6c67efd5. P1010003.jpg, 94724, 6576acb01fbd1b50373ce81a45492dd9c65a4c98e8ee75c3e04c1e6d9bec29bb. P1010004.jpg, 206201, fcac5f5fdc1190c21023ddd665ca91ff1fcad81157669e66179bdc3ab3f7b5f8. P1010005.jpg, 149612, 7d4ea9963f16bdecb741f5d3ad75af31ec641811a3dc34577638198ab168bbec. P1010006.jpg, 184480, edf9c5866cebb0dd7284a70fe01f218d940ab769075f71cd257d07d3a72197ac. P1010007.jpg, 163712, be80e16ba65497bf40cce29e3e15c5593fe069cdd289c5d441aeec07f04294b9. P1010008.jpg, 61155, cd7ea0235ca6253063bd2d28c7bf7dec62bdc52c91ae50873406c175d0b86951. P1010009.jpg, 79555, f98de276c083b48584e5a8f13a9482196fe545cdf2436fed95155bc86a5c3eea. P1010010.jpg, 81912, d24b75acb9d06910c7f49aacba9982e815b37afdbbc3589f5d9627fc6d695831. P1010011.jpg, 188033, 125fd7e651fe82f00b6f01957de009cd85d3ea33594e31b98064004f6b9df128. P1010012.jpg, 77987, 208eb6aed3e93a6c4965ecfca1193e263c4a7934d7fcea1a5dcf53deefe5ba62. P1010013.jpg, 168902, 9c6f78a4f3a5cd167f11995796b8cf5ff02ee5cc7dbeecb7ebf7e57195a38247. P1010014.jpg, 122861, fb453d0731f0c5553520742ecadcabec54a9a8d54393ae86dfc4098397def78f. P1010015.jpg, 156075, ff0c4d2cbaaaa24576d24c775e0e3294628bdc9af19eeca92560fbbc8e8cfde7. P1010016.jpg, 180671, 3eb318a632d6d5901f368dfceb11015367d2c6c3df3911a273fcd7caa0e55883. P1010017.jpg, 300811, 25b1917d2b95fd8ad7fb1eb895be58e623dbc1455aab7e89740020d4e90fda3d." .
_:r31b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r31b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r31b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r31b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r31b14 .
_:r31b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r31b13 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the staged photographs record" .
_:r31b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r31b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r31b14 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r31b14 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r31b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: held by the studio; reported, with one clock-independent bound. The seventeen files are held in the studio's photography folder, which sits beside the WTFIA folder on the same medium. That folder also holds the eleven frames of 2008-08-08 described in the photographs record, and one downloaded photograph by another photographer, which is not part of this series and not a studio original. The files' metadata is their own account and is not notarized, with one exception described below: a software version, which dates the files' processing without relying on anyone's clock. The images are not published. They show the face of a person who is not the studio principal and has not consented to publication, the face of a second, unidentified person, a readable vehicle registration plate and the exterior of a private residence. The SHA-256 checksum of every file is published below, so that the held files can later be shown to be unaltered without being disclosed." .
_:r31b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r31b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r31b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the frames show, in the camera's frame order. P1010001: a person poses at a white door, holding a ring of keys up to the camera. P1010002: the same person, inside a storage unit walled with wire mesh and crowded with boxes and a bicycle, holds up a pale cloth. P1010003: taken from behind, the person stands in the unit's doorway, a padlock hanging on the frame, and lifts the framed print. P1010004 and P1010005: the print's image, and the framed print propped upright, both lit by an on-camera flash. P1010006 and P1010007: the framed print leaning against a railing on the plank floor of a warehouse mezzanine. P1010008 and P1010009: the person, in the same space, holds the print squarely to the camera. P1010010 and P1010012: the verso, carrying the 'Photograph by Ansel Adams for The American Trust Company' stamp, the ink annotation 'Wells Fargo Bank', 'Negative No. 1-SF-46' and the title 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks', the same verso recorded in the photographs of 2008-08-08. P1010011: the person, in a corridor lined with chain-link storage cages, holds the print and looks into the camera. P1010013 and P1010014: in a framing workshop, an older man takes the print out of its frame at a workbench and then examines it on its mount. P1010015 to P1010017: a shed and carport in a garden, with fallen leaves on the grass." .
_:r31b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r31b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r31b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "How the series is dated, and how firmly. The camera, an Olympus C-3020Z, wrote no capture time: every DateTimeOriginal and DateTimeDigitized field reads 0000:00:00 00:00:00, which is what such a camera writes when its clock has never been set. The file names agree: this camera builds each name from the month and day, and 'P101' corresponds to 1 January, the date an unset clock reports. Every file was then rewritten by software, which left three fields: Software 'QuickTime 7.5.5', HostComputer 'Mac OS X 10.5.5', and a modification time running from 2008-11-25 18:56:10 to 18:56:17 in frame order, by that computer's clock, which records no timezone. Three bounds follow, of different strength. The floor is independent of any clock: QuickTime 7.5.5 was released on 2008-09-09 and Mac OS X 10.5.5 on 2008-09-15, so the files cannot have been processed by that pairing before 15 September 2008. The Mac's own date, 25 November 2008, falls within the months when 10.5.5 was the current release, before 10.5.6 arrived on 15 December 2008, and is consistent with it; it remains a clock reading, not a verified date. The ceiling is the file system's record of the copy into the studio's working folder, 2009-01-20 between 13:45 and 13:48, which is self-reported, and which falls two days before the WTFIA documents were begun by their own metadata. Two details in the frames agree with the autumn: fallen leaves lie on the grass in the exterior frames, and the workshop frames show a step the studio was planning in writing on 2008-11-20, as described below. None of this dates the capture itself, which could in principle precede the processing; it dates the files as they are held." .
_:r31b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r31b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r31b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The workshop frames, and a message five days earlier by the Mac's clock. On 2008-11-20 the studio wrote to the auction house's cataloguer about the shipment 'to Sotheby's in NYC for the upcoming spring Art auction', 'somewhat unsure' whether to keep the photograph in its original frame with its glass cover 'or have a professional art framer do the removal of the glass', as the Sotheby's correspondence record quotes. Frames P1010013 and P1010014 show the print being taken out of its frame at a framer's workbench and examined on its mount, in a workshop the studio identifies as Raven Frame Works, a framing business in Eugene, Oregon; the man at the workbench is not named. The same message gave a mailing address on Maui, and the studio identifies the person in the storage frames as its art assistant rather than its principal, so in these frames the print is being handled by others on the studio's behalf. The studio states that it directed the retrieval and the unframing remotely from Hawai'i, working with its art assistant and the framer, and that doing so was the composition of WTF is Ansel Adams. The documents bear out where the studio was and who handled the print; that the handling was the making of a work is the studio's account, given in 2026. The message and the files are both held by the studio, so neither is independent of the other's source. They are, however, two different kinds of record, one a message describing a plan and the other photographs of that plan being carried out, and they agree." .
_:r31b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r31b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r31b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The approach to the framer, and what it has produced so far. On 2024-09-05 the studio wrote to the proprietor of the framing business to ask whether he was the same person, and he replied the following day that he is. On 2024-09-06 the studio wrote again: it described the 2008 job, linked the auction house's public catalogue entry for lot 16, attached the workbench frame from this series, and asked whether the original receipt for the work survives. That second message was not answered. The studio wrote again on 2026-09-18, asking only whether the original receipts or any record of work done in 2008 survived, and was answered the same afternoon: 'I do not have any Raven Frame Works paperwork from 2008.' What the exchange establishes is therefore his identity, and a search of his own records that found nothing. It does not confirm the job, its date, or its purpose, and no record from the business has been produced. He has not been asked whether he wishes to be named, and is not named here. A surviving work order, or a few sentences of recollection in writing, would be the first account of any part of this series from outside the studio; until there is one, the series is dated by the studio's own files alone." .
_:r31b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r31b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r31b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The framer's answer, and exactly how far it reaches. The reply of 2026-09-18 is a negative finding and must be read as one. He says he holds no Raven Frame Works paperwork from 2008. He does not say that no work was done, that the print never came to his bench, or that he has no memory of it; he was asked about documents and he answered about documents. A small business not holding paperwork from eighteen years earlier is unremarkable and evidences nothing either way about the job. So the position after this exchange is the position before it, with one thing removed: the possibility that a surviving work order would settle the series' date from outside the studio. That possibility is now closed, and the series continues to rest on the studio's own files and on the clock-independent floor described above. What the exchange adds is small but real. He confirmed in 2024, in his own words, that he is the Raven Frame Works proprietor, and he has now answered a second time, so the business and the person the studio names are not a supposition. A few sentences of recollection in writing would still be the first account of any part of this series from outside the studio. He has not been asked for one, he has not been asked whether he wishes to be named, and he is not named here." .
_:r31b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r31b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r31b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A note on the evidential quality of that reply, because it is unlike everything else here. The message is held as an inbound file as the studio's mail provider received it, not as a copy from a sender's outbox. It therefore carries a full Received chain and, unusually for this collection, cryptographic authentication: the provider's own headers record dkim=pass against the sending domain's published key, spf=pass for the sending host, and arc=pass. A DKIM signature covers the message body and principal headers, so a passing result means the message was not altered between the sender's provider and the recipient's. Every message in the 2008 and 2009 correspondence is a Sent Items copy with no Received chain and no signature, and is trusted on the strength of circumstantial agreement. This one is verifiable in a way none of those are. The point is worth stating plainly and also worth bounding: what the signature authenticates is that this person sent this text on this date. It authenticates nothing about 2008." .
_:r31b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r31b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r31b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Who is pictured, and who held the camera. The person in the storage frames is identified by the studio as one of its art assistants; that name is withheld here pending their consent and is retained in the studio's private copy. The older man at the workbench is not identified in this record. The photographer is not pictured and is not named by the files, whose metadata carries no artist field. One frame, P1010003, is taken from behind the person pictured as they step into the storage unit, so at least that frame was made by someone other than its subject. Sun & Rain Works states that the series was photographed by its art assistants." .
_:r31b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r31b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r32b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: www.wikioccupy.org, the studio's Occupy wiki, in its chat logs and a painting (November 2011)" .
_:r32b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r32b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r32b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r32b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-wikioccupy-2011" .
_:r32b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r32b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r32b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status. The logs are held as three rich-text files, 11-10-11WikiChat.rtf, 32,280 bytes, SHA-256 ca1f7fcceb4c1be172ed5d2d5fecf26d523785912e9e1193f45e2e8d0154b157; 11-19-11GlobalWikiChat.rtf, 70,274 bytes, SHA-256 ab3e8f66cf3b3a39d29e819efee06b26892906ce93e528a076fc9902ce02a30b; and AeonJaggoffChatwiki11-19-11.rtf, 3,793 bytes, SHA-256 79056e5c83f20a7216c362803534ea2869525d74f451ed2e538f93a787e7b79f. They are the studio's own saved copies of a conversation with other people, and their lines carry times but no dates; the dates are given by the file names. They are dated more firmly by a message the studio principal sent from his mailbox on 2012-11-12 at 19:43:53 Pacific time, under the subject 'Art Historical Archives', to a private correspondent, which carries the three files as attachments, byte-identical to the copies now held, and which bears a message identifier of the form the Windows Live Hotmail service issued at that time. The logs therefore existed, in their present form, by 2012-11-13 at 03:43 UTC. One further check is internal: the channel topic recorded at the head of the 19 November log announces a meeting on 'Wednesday Nov. 16', and 2011-11-16 was a Wednesday. The logs are not published, because nearly all of their text is other people's; their checksums are, so that the held files can later be shown to be unaltered. The message of 2011-11-01 is in the account data Facebook compiled and delivered on request on 2026-09-24, held as facebook-joechirum-2026-09-24-TZ87gGfq.zip, SHA-256 c978c3bd338a6a9a934d3aa05a81d2c298c49200bf7e4edc98b8329c02103097, in a thread file of 1,756 bytes, SHA-256 45be57affddbfaaa95fd244dbfc371872ab3634831d7017727c7bb2831c27be8; its date is the one Facebook's servers assigned. The photograph is the camera's original, a JPEG of 2,048 by 1,536 pixels with its Exif block intact, held as 1116110742.JPEG, 471,800 bytes, SHA-256 ca9930c7d37af9bb1e86299d5b1165f2f1523cf83af9c76f1e8e1a224fa39d7d, and is published here at /images/wikioccupy-painting-2011.jpg as a byte-identical copy, renamed for the web and not otherwise altered." .
_:r32b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r32b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r32b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "How the photograph is dated. The Exif block names the camera as an LG Electronics LG-VX8575 with firmware VX857V06, the camera phone that made the photograph of 2011-11-08 set out in the inscription photograph record, and gives DateTimeOriginal as 2011:11:16 07:42:37. The file name, 1116110742, is the phone's own naming from month, day, two-digit year, hour and minute, and gives the same minute. The file carries no GPS data. The same phone photographed other studio works in the same half-hour that morning, among them the painted postcard of 07:29 set out in the postcard record. The date is the file's own account and is not notarized, and it dates the photograph, not the painting's making; the painting existed by that morning. No title for the painting is recorded here, and whether it survives is not stated." .
_:r32b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r32b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r32b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The site itself, as archived. The Internet Archive first reached www.wikioccupy.org on 2011-10-24, when the address redirected into the wiki; pages of the running wiki were captured on 2011-10-25, and its main page on 2011-10-31 at 01:35:40 UTC, at https://web.archive.org/web/20111031013540/http://www.wikioccupy.org/index.php?title=Main_Page . That page, last modified on 30 October 2011, is titled 'Wiki Occupy Home', names its software in its own header as MediaWiki 1.17.0, the software of Wikipedia, and describes the site as 'an unofficial collective wiki for all of the occupy groups meeting up across the United States', and gives as its contact an address at the site's own domain under the name 'Art', the name the studio principal used in the chat. On 2011-12-03 the address returned only the hosting provider's default page. As captured, the site presents itself as a wiki for the Occupy movement and does not describe itself as a work of art; the art framing is in the studio principal's words in the chat, not on the site." .
_:r32b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r32b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r32b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What these establish, and what they do not. They establish that on 2011-11-01, by Facebook's clock, the studio principal called himself the creator and administrator of www.wikioccupy.org and used the name 'Art' in his contact address for it. They also establish that in November 2011, in a conversation with other people and at a date fixed by a message of 2012, the studio principal described his holding of the wikioccupy domains 'as an act of Art' and the project as one Art project among others in his life, that he placed the domain under his art business, and that by 2011-11-16 he had made a painting whose subject is the site's address. Three limits go with that. The name he used in the channel was 'Art', so some of his phrases can be read either way: 'Art's project' may mean the project of the person called Art, and 'happening' may be meant in the ordinary sense of taking place, although the studio used the word of its own work elsewhere, as the archived-websites record sets out for 'Art Market Reform is a happening'. The line 'as an act of Art' follows 'i was/am very enthusiastic about the wiki occupy idea' and is quoted in sequence so that the reader can judge what it refers to. And the logs do not name Metaconceptual Art or mention www.wtfisart.com, Sotheby's or the sale of 2009, so the connections the studio draws, to Metaconceptual Art and to www.wtfisart.com as the source of the idea of a website as art, rest on its own account, given in 2026. Two dated facts sit close to that account without proving it: the studio principal's earliest public use of the word metaconceptual, in a posting of 2011-11-28 set out in the record of the 2011-2012 postings, falls nine days after the log of 19 November; and www.wtfisart.com, the earlier website the studio names as the source of the idea, was registered by the studio on 2009-03-16, as the registrar's receipts show. What this record adds, independently of the studio's account, is that in 2011 the studio did describe a website it ran, and its holding of that website's names, as art. It bears on no date before 2011." .
_:r32b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r32b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r32b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Redaction and scope. Only the studio principal's own words are quoted. The other participants in the channel, the network addresses the logs record, the person to whom the message of 2011-11-01 was sent, the contact address it gives, and the correspondent to whom the logs were sent in 2012 are not named or given, and nothing any of them wrote is reproduced. No endorsement of this collection, of Metaconceptual Art or of any claim here by any person in the channel, or by the Occupy movement, is implied. The domain names are given because they are the subject of the record; both principal domains have since lapsed." .
_:r32b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r32b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r32b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r32b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r32b8 .
_:r32b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r32b7 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the wikioccupy.org record" .
_:r32b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r32b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r32b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r32b8 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r33b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> .
_:r33b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P75i_is_possessed_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r33b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E30_Right> .
_:r33b0 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "CC BY 4.0" .
_:r33b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: the WTFIA source folder for www.wtfisart.com (2008-2009)" .
_:r33b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r33b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r33b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r33b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-wtfia-2009" .
_:r33b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r33b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r33b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: studio-held, with self-describing file metadata. The dates given here are drawn from filesystem modification times and from the OLE document-summary metadata embedded in the Word files. They are internally consistent across the ninety-seven files in the folder, but they are reported by the files themselves and are not independently notarized; they should be read as the archive's own account of its dates. The press sources excerpted in 'IsItArt.doc' are independently verifiable at their published URLs. No checksums, file hashes, or custody identifiers are published at this time. The same storage medium also holds an unrelated 2009 website project on renewable energy, 'Growing Aloha'; it is outside the boundary of this evidence archive." .
_:r33b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r33b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r33b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Embedded document metadata, as recorded by the files themselves: 'IsItArt.doc' gives author Joseph M. Chirum, created 2009-01-22T03:03, last saved 2009-01-28T21:23, revision 21, total editing time 09:46, 14 pages, 4,049 words, 23,082 characters; its Title field carries the string 'August 13, 2007', which is a metadata field value and not, by itself, evidence of a 2007 document. That string is now accounted for: 'August 13, 2007' is the date of one of the twenty-four press reports compiled inside the document itself, a New York Sun piece by Kate Taylor headed 'Seeking A Hedge For Art'. The Title field took the value of a heading in the body, as Word does, and carries no authorship date of its own. 'Photographers.doc' gives author Joseph M. Chirum, created 2009-01-22T04:19, last saved 2009-01-26T04:15, revision 4, 1 page, 69 words. 'OldMasters.doc' gives created 2009-01-23T18:22, last saved 2009-01-26T20:50, revision 19, 8 pages, 1,263 words. 'Modern.doc' gives created 2009-01-27T13:15, last saved 2009-01-28T03:49, revision 10, and was produced with a different word processor from the other three. A second contributor is recorded in the metadata of all four documents: as the named author of both dealer-network directories, 'OldMasters.doc' and 'Modern.doc', and as the last editor of all four files. That person's name is withheld from this public record pending their consent, and is retained in the studio's private copy; the attribution of the two directories is theirs, not the studio principal's." .
_:r33b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r33b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r33b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The folder is named for the 'WTF is Art' concept and holds the material Sun & Rain Works assembled for www.wtfisart.com: the art-market press compilation, the photographer note, and the two dealer-network directories of galleries and their web addresses that were built as the site's link content. Its art-market compilation is the documented source context for the image study Art Market Reform, and the dealer directories are a documented instance of the project's stated interest in an archaeology of the art world. The folder holds no HTML: the built pages of www.wtfisart.com are not among these files, so the folder evidences what was made for the site, not what the site published or when it went live. The folder also contains an Ansel Adams reference image dated 2009-01-21 and a photographer reference note dated 2009-01-25; these show that Ansel Adams and photographic authorship were present in the same 2009 working set, and nothing further. The 2009 Sotheby's consignment is evidenced separately, in the auction-engagement evidence record. One temporal alignment is worth recording: the latest-dated material in the folder - the modern-gallery directory, last saved 2009-03-23, and the seventeen art-market images, all dated 2009-03-23 - falls one week before the Sotheby's Photographs sale of 30 March 2009 at which lot 16 was offered. The folder was therefore in active use in the week before that sale. This places the work and the sale in the same period; it does not show that the material was made for the sale, and no such intent is claimed from the dates alone. Each item here is scoped to what it shows." .
_:r33b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r33b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r33b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Contents of the documents, as counted in 2026 when they were transcribed in full: 'IsItArt.doc' holds twenty-four dated press reports running from 13 May 2000 to 29 January 2009, twenty-two of them carrying the source link the compiler recorded and two carrying none; 'OldMasters.doc' holds sixty-four dealers and galleries, every one with a web address, grouped under seventeen regional headings; 'Modern.doc' holds eighty-one galleries under twenty-nine regional headings with no web addresses at all, its first group of five carrying no heading; 'Photographers.doc' holds the single note on Lee Balterman. These counts describe the documents as held and are independent of any claim about the website." .
_:r33b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r33b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r33b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "An exhaustive negative search, run 2026-09-01 and recorded so that it need not be repeated. All ninety-eight files on the studio's source medium, totalling 20,271,324 bytes, were searched for any trace of the 2009 auction engagement. The method was not a reading of the readable files but an extraction of every ASCII and every UTF-16 string from every byte of every file, so that text embedded in the Word documents' internal streams, in Photoshop layers, in the thumbnail caches and in the images' own metadata segments would be caught. The following terms return no hit anywhere in the folder: consign, consignment, N08533, lot 16, Twin Peaks, appraisal, hammer, estimate, and the surname of either member of Sotheby's staff. So do the terms metaconceptual, meta conceptual, wtfisart, WTF is Art, and Sun and Rain, none of which appears in the folder at all, including in the folder named for the first of them. The word Sotheby appears only inside the press compilation, in the headline and body of art-market reports about the auction houses, and the word auction likewise, together with one gallery whose trading name contains it. The words Ansel and Adams appear only in the filename of a reference plate and in a shortcut created in 2026. The conclusion is the one this record already stated and can now state as tested rather than assumed: the working material assembled for www.wtfisart.com, worked on until the week before the sale of 30 March 2009, contains no mention of the consignment, of the sale, or of the concept later called Metaconceptual Art." .
_:r33b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r33b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r33b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r33b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r33b9 .
